OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Kadisons-similarity-theorem-through-uniform-derivation-estimates-September-23-2026", "title": "Kadison's similarity theorem through uniform derivation estimates", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Kadisons-similarity-theorem-through-uniform-derivation-estimates-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "288", "family_title": "Kadison's similarity conjecture", "abstract_html": "We prove that every bounded complex-linear unital algebra homomorphism from a unital complex <i>C</i><sup>∗</sup>-algebra into the bounded operators on an arbitrary Hilbert space is similar to a \\(*\\)-homomorphism. This resolves Kadison's similarity conjecture positively. We also obtain one universal hyperreflexivity constant for all unital von Neumann algebras on arbitrary complex Hilbert spaces.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an operator \\(S\\in B(H)\\), invertible with bounded inverse, such that \\( \\rho(a)=S\\pi(a)S^{-1}\\qquad(a\\in A) \\) is a \\(*\\)-homomorphism.", "statement": "<p>**Similarity — original Theorem 1.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(A\\) is a unital complex \\(C^*\\)-algebra.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(H\\) is an arbitrary complex Hilbert space.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\pi:A\\to B(H)\\) is a bounded, complex-linear, unital algebra homomorphism.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an operator \\(S\\in B(H)\\), invertible with bounded inverse, such that\n\\[\n\\rho(a)=S\\pi(a)S^{-1}\\qquad(a\\in A)\n\\]\nis a \\(*\\)-homomorphism. In particular,\n\\[\n\\rho(a^*)=\\rho(a)^*\\qquad(a\\in A).\n\\]\nNo separability, nuclearity, finite generation, faithfulness, or normality condition is imposed.</p>", "proof": "<p>For every faithful unital \\(*\\)-representation of \\(A\\), Proposition 10 makes every bounded derivation into the ambient bounded operators inner. Thus condition 2 of Proposition 2 holds for \\(A\\).</p><p>Kirchberg’s equivalent similarity property gives, for the bounded unital homomorphism \\(\\pi:A\\to B(H)\\), a bounded invertible \\(S\\) with bounded inverse such that\n\\[\nS\\pi(\\cdot)S^{-1}\n\\]\nis a \\(*\\)-homomorphism. The identity isomorphism treats the zero Hilbert space as well.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an absolute constant \\(C<\\infty\\), independent of \\(P,K,Y,h\\), such that \\( \\|[Y^{(h)},X]\\|\\le Cg_P(Y)\\|X\\| \\qquad(h\\ge 1,\\ X\\in M_h(P)). \\)", "statement": "<p>**Uniform commutator estimate — original Theorem 1.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(K\\) is an arbitrary complex Hilbert space.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\subset B(K)\\) is a unital von Neumann algebra.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(Y\\in B(K)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\ng_P(Y)=\\sup\\{\\|Ya-aY\\|:a\\in P,\\ \\|a\\|\\le 1\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(h\\ge 1\\), \\(Y^{(h)}\\) denotes the diagonal amplification of \\(Y\\) on \\(K^h\\), and \\(M_h(P)\\) acts naturally on \\(K^h\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\([T,U]=TU-UT\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an absolute constant \\(C&lt;\\infty\\), independent of \\(P,K,Y,h\\), such that\n\\[\n\\|[Y^{(h)},X]\\|\\le Cg_P(Y)\\|X\\|\n\\qquad(h\\ge 1,\\ X\\in M_h(P)).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the numerical constant\n\\[\nC=3C_{\\mathrm{fac}}+2,\\qquad\nC_{\\mathrm{fac}}\n=\n\\max\\{3c_1+2,\\ 1+2c_{\\mathrm{row}},\\ 2\\}.\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(h\\ge1\\), \\(X\\in M_h(P)\\), and a unit vector\n\\[\n\\xi=(\\xi_1,\\ldots,\\xi_h)\\in K^h.\n\\]\nProposition 9 gives a separable common reducing subspace \\(K_0\\), containing the components of \\(\\xi\\), and operators\n\\[\nY_0=Y|_{K_0},\\qquad\nP_0=\\sigma(A_X)''\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\ng_{P_0}(Y_0)\\le g_P(Y),\\qquad\n\\|\\sigma_h(X)\\|\\le\\|X\\|.\n\\]</p><p>Apply Proposition 8 on \\(K_0\\):\n\\[\n\\|[Y_0^{(h)},\\sigma_h(X)]\\|\n\\le\nCg_{P_0}(Y_0)\\|\\sigma_h(X)\\|\n\\le Cg_P(Y)\\|X\\|.\n\\]\nThe restricted commutator on \\(\\xi\\) equals the original one, so\n\\[\n\\|[Y^{(h)},X]\\xi\\|\n\\le Cg_P(Y)\\|X\\|.\n\\]\nTaking the supremum over unit vectors \\(\\xi\\) proves (1). If \\(K=\\{0\\}\\), the estimate is vacuous.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(T\\in B(H)\\), \\( \\operatorname{dist}(T,M)\\le 2C\\alpha_M(T). \\) Thus every unital von Neumann algebra is hyperreflexive with constant at most \\(2C\\), independently of the algebra, its representation, and the …", "statement": "<p>**Universal hyperreflexivity — original Corollary 1.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(H\\) is an arbitrary complex Hilbert space.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(M\\subset B(H)\\) is a unital von Neumann algebra.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(T\\in B(H)\\), define\n\\[\n\\alpha_M(T)=\n\\sup\\{\\|(1-e)Te\\|:e\\in M',\\ e=e^*=e^2\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(C\\) is the absolute constant in Theorem 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(T\\in B(H)\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(T,M)\\le 2C\\alpha_M(T).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nThus every unital von Neumann algebra is hyperreflexive with constant at most \\(2C\\), independently of the algebra, its representation, and the Hilbert space. No optimality of this constant is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(P=M'\\). Since \\(M\\) is self-adjoint, its invariant subspaces are reducing, and their orthogonal projections are exactly the projections in \\(P\\). Thus \\(\\alpha_M\\) is the usual off-diagonal distance seminorm.</p><p>For a projection \\(e\\in P\\), the two off-diagonal blocks give\n\\[\n\\|[T,e]\\|\n=\n\\max\\{\\|(1-e)Te\\|,\\|eT(1-e)\\|\\}\n\\le\\alpha_M(T),\n\\]\nwhere the last inequality also uses \\(1-e\\).</p><p>Spectral step approximations express a self-adjoint contraction \\(a\\in P\\), modulo a scalar, as a norm limit of linear combinations of projections in \\(P\\) whose coefficients have total absolute value at most two. Hence\n\\[\n\\|[T,a]\\|\\le2\\alpha_M(T).\n\\]\nThe real and imaginary parts of a contraction in \\(P\\) are self-adjoint contractions, so\n\\[\ng_P(T)\\le4\\alpha_M(T).\n\\]</p><p>Arveson’s commutant-distance formula, in the form recalled in [7, Equation (2.1)], states\n\\[\n2\\operatorname{dist}(T,M)\n=\n\\|\\operatorname{ad}_T|_P\\|_{\\mathrm{cb}},\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{ad}_T(a)=[T,a],\n\\]\nbecause \\(P'=M\\).</p><p>Taking the supremum over finite matrix sizes in Theorem 2 yields\n\\[\n2\\operatorname{dist}(T,M)\n\\le Cg_P(T)\n\\le4C\\alpha_M(T).\n\\]\nDivision by two proves the asserted universal hyperreflexivity estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every bounded rectangular derivation satisfies \\( \\max\\{\\|\\Delta\\|_{\\mathrm{row}},\\|\\Delta\\|_{\\mathrm{col}}\\} \\le c_{\\mathrm{row}}\\|\\Delta\\|. \\) If \\(\\Delta\\) is completely bounded, then there is \\(V\\in B(K,F)\\) such …", "statement": "<p>**Rectangular derivations: row and column estimates, completely bounded implementation, and cyclic-domain implementation — original Lemmas 2.1, 2.2, and 2.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">All Hilbert spaces are complex, with inner products linear in the first variable.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(A\\) is a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\sigma:A\\to B(K)\\) and \\(\\lambda:A\\to B(F)\\) are unital \\(*\\)-representations.</p><p class=\"li\">A rectangular derivation is a linear map \\(\\Delta:A\\to B(K,F)\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\Delta(ab)=\\Delta(a)\\sigma(b)+\\lambda(a)\\Delta(b)\n\\qquad(a,b\\in A).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(\\Delta(1)=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(u:A\\to B(K,F)\\), \\(u_{p,q}\\) denotes entrywise amplification on \\(M_{p,q}(A)\\), with values in \\(B(K^q,F^p)\\). Write \\(u_h=u_{h,h}\\), and define\n\\[\n\\|u\\|_{\\mathrm{cb}}=\\sup_{h\\ge1}\\|u_h\\|,\\qquad\n\\|u\\|_{\\mathrm{row}}=\\sup_{h\\ge1}\\|u_{1,h}\\|,\\qquad\n\\|u\\|_{\\mathrm{col}}=\\sup_{h\\ge1}\\|u_{h,1}\\|.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n\\[\nc_{\\mathrm{row}}=4\\sqrt2,\\qquad\nc_0=(1+4c_{\\mathrm{row}})^2.\n\\tag{5,9}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. Every bounded rectangular derivation satisfies\n\\[\n\\max\\{\\|\\Delta\\|_{\\mathrm{row}},\\|\\Delta\\|_{\\mathrm{col}}\\}\n\\le c_{\\mathrm{row}}\\|\\Delta\\|.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(\\Delta\\) is completely bounded, then there is \\(V\\in B(K,F)\\) such that\n\\[\n\\Delta(a)=V\\sigma(a)-\\lambda(a)V\\qquad(a\\in A).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\nIf \\(d=\\|\\Delta\\|&gt;0\\), one may choose\n\\[\n\\|V\\|\\le d\\bigl(1+\\|\\Delta\\|_{\\mathrm{cb}}/d\\bigr)^2.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\Delta=0\\), one may take \\(V=0\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If the domain representation \\(\\sigma\\) has a cyclic vector, every bounded rectangular derivation is completely bounded, with\n\\[\n\\|\\Delta\\|_{\\mathrm{cb}}\\le4c_{\\mathrm{row}}\\|\\Delta\\|,\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\nand has an implementer satisfying\n\\[\n\\Delta(a)=V\\sigma(a)-\\lambda(a)V,\\qquad\n\\|V\\|\\le c_0\\|\\Delta\\|.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\nThe output representation \\(\\lambda\\) may have arbitrary multiplicity.</p>", "proof": "<p>The first two assertions are Lemmas 1.1 and 1.2. For the cyclic-domain assertion, zero spaces and the zero derivation are immediate, so assume they do not occur.</p><p>Put \\(P=\\sigma(A)''\\). Fix \\(h\\ge1\\), \\(X\\in M_h(A)\\) with \\(\\|X\\|\\le1\\), and a unit vector \\(\\xi\\in K^h\\). Since \\(\\sigma\\) has a cyclic vector, Lemma 1.3 supplies a unit vector \\(\\zeta\\in K\\) and a contraction\n\\[\nv\\in M_{h,1}(P),\\qquad v\\zeta=\\xi.\n\\]</p><p>Matrix Kaplansky density gives column contractions\n\\[\nz_\\alpha\\in M_{h,1}(\\sigma(A))\n\\]\nconverging strongly to \\(v\\). For the rectangular assertion, regard \\(v\\) as the first column of an element of \\(M_h(P)\\), with all other columns zero; approximate strongly by contractions in \\(M_h(\\sigma(A))\\), and compress to the first input coordinate.</p><p>The \\(*\\)-image \\(\\sigma(A)\\) is the \\(C^*\\)-quotient \\(A/\\ker\\sigma\\), and the same identification holds at every square matrix level. Thus each \\(z_\\alpha\\) lifts to \\(w_\\alpha\\in M_{h,1}(A)\\) such that\n\\[\n\\|w_\\alpha\\|\\le2,\\qquad\n\\sigma_{h,1}(w_\\alpha)=z_\\alpha,\\qquad\n\\sigma_{h,1}(w_\\alpha)\\zeta\\longrightarrow\\xi.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\nFor example, lift the square matrix with first column \\(z_\\alpha\\) and all other columns zero to a matrix of norm less than \\(2\\), then compress its first column.</p><p>Entrywise application of the derivation identity gives\n\\[\n\\Delta_h(X)\\sigma_{h,1}(w_\\alpha)\n=\n\\Delta_{h,1}(Xw_\\alpha)\n-\\lambda_h(X)\\Delta_{h,1}(w_\\alpha).\n\\]\nSince \\(\\lambda\\) is completely contractive, Lemma 1.1 yields\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\Delta_h(X)\\sigma_{h,1}(w_\\alpha)\\zeta\\|\n&amp;\\le\nc_{\\mathrm{row}}\\|\\Delta\\|\n\\bigl(\\|Xw_\\alpha\\|+\\|\\lambda_h(X)\\|\\|w_\\alpha\\|\\bigr)\\\\\n&amp;\\le4c_{\\mathrm{row}}\\|\\Delta\\|.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAt the fixed finite level \\(h\\), \\(\\Delta_h(X)\\) is already a bounded operator. Taking the limit gives\n\\[\n\\|\\Delta_h(X)\\xi\\|\\le4c_{\\mathrm{row}}\\|\\Delta\\|.\n\\]\nTaking the supremum over \\(\\xi,X,h\\) proves the completely bounded estimate.</p><p>The quotient argument did not require \\(\\Delta\\) to vanish on \\(\\ker\\sigma\\); the derivation identity was applied to the chosen lifts in \\(A\\). Lemma 1.2 now gives\n\\[\n\\|V\\|\\le\\|\\Delta\\|(1+4c_{\\mathrm{row}})^2=c_0\\|\\Delta\\|.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\max\\{\\|\\Delta\\|_{\\mathrm{row}},\\|\\Delta\\|_{\\mathrm{col}}\\} \\le c_{\\mathrm{row}}\\|\\Delta\\|. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Row and column bounds — original Lemma 2.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(A,\\sigma,\\lambda,\\Delta\\) satisfy the rectangular derivation assumptions of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\Delta\\) is bounded; no cyclicity assumption is made.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\max\\{\\|\\Delta\\|_{\\mathrm{row}},\\|\\Delta\\|_{\\mathrm{col}}\\}\n\\le c_{\\mathrm{row}}\\|\\Delta\\|.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Dickson’s row estimate states that every bounded unital homomorphism\n\\(\\phi:A\\to B(\\mathcal H)\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\|\\phi\\|_{\\mathrm{row}}\\le\\sqrt2\\,\\|\\phi\\|^2\n\\]\n[8, Lemma 2.9, Equation (19)].</p><p>The map\n\\[\n\\phi^\\sharp(a)=\\phi(a^*)^*\n\\]\nis complex-linear, has the same norm as \\(\\phi\\), and is a homomorphism, since\n\\[\n\\phi^\\sharp(ab)\n=\\bigl(\\phi(b^*)\\phi(a^*)\\bigr)^*\n=\\phi^\\sharp(a)\\phi^\\sharp(b).\n\\]\nApply the row estimate to \\(\\phi^\\sharp\\) on\n\\([a_1^*,\\ldots,a_h^*]\\), and take the rectangular adjoint. This gives the same bound for \\(\\|\\phi\\|_{\\mathrm{col}}\\). Consequently both norms are at most \\(c_{\\mathrm{row}}=4\\sqrt2\\) when \\(\\|\\phi\\|\\le2\\).</p><p>The assertion is immediate when \\(\\Delta=0\\). Otherwise put \\(d=\\|\\Delta\\|&gt;0\\) and define\n\\[\n\\Phi(a)=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n\\lambda(a)&amp;d^{-1}\\Delta(a)\\\\\n0&amp;\\sigma(a)\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\in B(F\\oplus K).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\nThe derivation identity makes \\(\\Phi\\) unital and multiplicative. Its diagonal map is contractive and its off-diagonal map has norm one, so \\(\\|\\Phi\\|\\le2\\).</p><p>The off-diagonal rectangular compression of \\(\\Phi_{p,q}\\), from \\(K^q\\) to \\(F^p\\), is \\(d^{-1}\\Delta_{p,q}\\). The row and column estimates for \\(\\Phi\\) therefore give the result.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is \\(V\\in B(K,F)\\) such that \\( \\Delta(a)=V\\sigma(a)-\\lambda(a)V\\qquad(a\\in A). \\) If \\(d>0\\), one may choose \\( \\|V\\|\\le d\\bigl(1+\\|\\Delta\\|_{\\mathrm{cb}}/d\\bigr)^2. \\) If \\(d=0\\), take \\(V=0\\).", "statement": "<p>**Completely bounded rectangular derivations — original Lemma 2.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(A,\\sigma,\\lambda,\\Delta\\) satisfy the rectangular derivation assumptions of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\Delta\\) is completely bounded.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(d=\\|\\Delta\\|\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(V\\in B(K,F)\\) such that\n\\[\n\\Delta(a)=V\\sigma(a)-\\lambda(a)V\\qquad(a\\in A).\n\\]\nIf \\(d&gt;0\\), one may choose\n\\[\n\\|V\\|\\le d\\bigl(1+\\|\\Delta\\|_{\\mathrm{cb}}/d\\bigr)^2.\n\\]\nIf \\(d=0\\), take \\(V=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the triangular-metric argument recalled in Ozawa [18, Lemma 1.2], allowing different domain and codomain representations and keeping track of the implementer norm.</p><p>For \\(\\Delta=0\\), take \\(V=0\\). Otherwise the triangular homomorphism\n\\[\n\\Phi(a)=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n\\lambda(a)&amp;d^{-1}\\Delta(a)\\\\\n0&amp;\\sigma(a)\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nis completely bounded, with\n\\[\n\\|\\Phi\\|_{\\mathrm{cb}}\n\\le1+\\|\\Delta\\|_{\\mathrm{cb}}/d=:G.\n\\]\nPaulsen’s completely bounded similarity theorem [19, p. 226] supplies an invertible \\(S\\) such that \\(S\\Phi(\\cdot)S^{-1}\\) is completely contractive and\n\\[\n\\|S\\|\\|S^{-1}\\|\\le G.\n\\]\nA unital completely contractive homomorphism from a \\(C^*\\)-algebra is a \\(*\\)-homomorphism: a unitary and its inverse are sent to contractions, hence its image is unitary, and unitaries linearly span the algebra.</p><p>Put \\(D=S^*S\\). Preservation of adjoints is equivalent to\n\\[\nD\\Phi(a)=\\Phi(a^*)^*D\\qquad(a\\in A).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\nWrite \\(D=(D_{ij})_{i,j=1}^2\\) relative to \\(F\\oplus K\\). The first row gives\n\\[\nD_{11}\\lambda(a)=\\lambda(a)D_{11},\n\\]\n\\[\nd^{-1}D_{11}\\Delta(a)+D_{12}\\sigma(a)\n=\\lambda(a)D_{12}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\nSince \\(D\\ge\\|D^{-1}\\|^{-1}I\\), its compression \\(D_{11}\\) is positive invertible, with\n\\[\n\\|D_{11}^{-1}\\|\\le\\|D^{-1}\\|.\n\\]\nIts inverse commutes with \\(\\lambda(A)\\). Therefore\n\\[\nV=-dD_{11}^{-1}D_{12}\n\\]\nhas the required implementation identity, and\n\\[\n\\|V\\|\n\\le d\\|D^{-1}\\|\\|D\\|\n=d\\bigl(\\|S^{-1}\\|\\|S\\|\\bigr)^2\n\\le dG^2.\n\\]\nIf either Hilbert space is zero, then \\(\\Delta=0\\), which has already been covered.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are a unit vector \\(\\zeta\\in K\\) and a contraction \\( v=(v_i)_{i=1}^h\\in M_{h,1}(P) \\) such that \\( \\xi_i=v_i\\zeta\\qquad(1\\le i\\le h). \\)", "statement": "<p>**Column factorization in a cyclic representation — original Lemma 2.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\subset B(K)\\) is a unital von Neumann algebra with a cyclic vector.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(h\\ge1\\) is an integer.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\xi=(\\xi_1,\\ldots,\\xi_h)\\in K^h\\) is a unit vector.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are a unit vector \\(\\zeta\\in K\\) and a contraction\n\\[\nv=(v_i)_{i=1}^h\\in M_{h,1}(P)\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n\\xi_i=v_i\\zeta\\qquad(1\\le i\\le h).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(N=P'\\), and choose a cyclic vector \\(\\Omega\\) for \\(P\\). It separates \\(N\\): if \\(x\\in N\\) and \\(x\\Omega=0\\), then\n\\[\nxa\\Omega=ax\\Omega=0\\qquad(a\\in P),\n\\]\nso \\(x=0\\).</p><p>Let \\(L=\\overline{N\\Omega}\\subset K\\). This subspace reduces \\(N\\), and restriction \\(r:N\\to B(L)\\) is faithful and normal. Indeed, an element of its kernel annihilates \\(\\Omega\\), and normality follows from compression to a reducing subspace. Thus \\(r\\) is a normal \\(*\\)-isomorphism onto the von Neumann algebra \\(r(N)\\), with normal inverse. The vector \\(\\Omega\\) is cyclic and separating for \\(r(N)\\).</p><p>The standard-form theorem for a representation with a cyclic separating vector realizes every normal positive functional on \\(r(N)\\) as a vector functional, with its vector in the natural cone [11, Lemma 2.10(1)]. Apply it, through \\(r\\), to\n\\[\n\\omega(x)=\\sum_{i=1}^h\\langle x\\xi_i,\\xi_i\\rangle\n\\qquad(x\\in N).\n\\]\nThere is \\(\\zeta\\in L\\subset K\\) such that\n\\[\n\\langle x\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle=\\omega(x)\\qquad(x\\in N).\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n\\|\\zeta\\|^2=\\omega(1)=1.\n\\]</p><p>The assignment\n\\[\nx\\zeta\\longmapsto(x\\xi_1,\\ldots,x\\xi_h)\n\\]\nis isometric, since both squared norms equal \\(\\omega(x^*x)\\). Extend it to an isometry on \\(\\overline{N\\zeta}\\), and then by zero on its orthogonal complement to a contraction \\(v:K\\to K^h\\).</p><p>The subspace \\(\\overline{N\\zeta}\\) reduces \\(N\\), and the isometry intertwines the \\(N\\)-actions. Hence\n\\[\nvx=x^{(h)}v\\qquad(x\\in N).\n\\]\nEach coordinate \\(v_i\\) lies in \\(N'=P\\), and \\(v\\zeta=\\xi\\). The constructions and the standard-form assertion are valid on nonseparable Hilbert spaces.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The following properties are equivalent.", "statement": "<p>**Kirchberg’s similarity–derivation equivalence — original Theorem 2.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(A\\) is a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The following properties are equivalent.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Every bounded unital homomorphism \\(\\pi:A\\to B(H)\\), on any Hilbert space \\(H\\), is similar by a bounded invertible operator to a \\(*\\)-homomorphism.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For every faithful unital \\(*\\)-representation \\(\\sigma:A\\to B(K)\\), on any Hilbert space \\(K\\), every bounded linear map \\(\\Delta:A\\to B(K)\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\Delta(ab)=\\Delta(a)\\sigma(b)+\\sigma(a)\\Delta(b)\n\\]\nhas the form\n\\[\n\\Delta(a)=V\\sigma(a)-\\sigma(a)V\n\\qquad(a\\in A)\n\\]\nfor some \\(V\\in B(K)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>This is Kirchberg’s theorem [16], in the per-algebra formulation with every faithful representation stated in Ozawa [18, Theorem 1.1].</p><p>The usual derivation property is stated without a continuity hypothesis. Ringrose’s automatic continuity theorem for derivations from \\(C^*\\)-algebras into Banach bimodules [27, Theorem 2] makes it equivalent to the bounded version above.</p><p>The implementation argument in Proposition 1 uses only Paulsen’s established completely bounded similarity theorem, not this unrestricted similarity conclusion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "With \\( c_1=6\\pi^2(1+4c_0), \\) one has \\( \\left\\| P_{L^2(A_0)}[G,L_s]\\big|_{L^2(A_0)} \\right\\| \\le c_1\\epsilon. \\) The constant is independent of the trace \\(t\\) of the corner.", "statement": "<p>**Free-product corner estimate — original Theorem 3.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(D\\) is a \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor generated as a von Neumann algebra by an increasing sequence \\((D_j)\\) of unital full matrix subalgebras whose sizes tend to infinity.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(A_0\\) is a finite von Neumann algebra with a faithful normal tracial state.</p><p class=\"li\">Form the tracial von Neumann free product\n\\[\nL=D*A_0,\n\\]\nand write \\(\\tau\\) for its trace and its restrictions.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(H=L^2(L,\\tau)\\), with\n\\[\n\\langle x,y\\rangle=\\tau(y^*x),\\qquad\n\\|x\\|_2=\\tau(x^*x)^{1/2},\\qquad\n\\|x\\|_1=\\tau(|x|).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Left and right multiplication are denoted by \\(L_a\\) and \\(R_a\\), with \\(R_a\\xi=\\xi a\\), so \\(R_aR_b=R_{ba}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The tracial commutation theorem identifies \\(L(L)'\\) with \\(\\{R_b:b\\in L\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The left and right supports of an \\(L^2\\)-vector are the support projections obtained from its affiliated-operator polar decomposition; they are equivalent and have the same trace.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(t=1/N\\), where \\(N\\ge2\\) is an integer.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(G\\in B(H)\\) commutes with \\(L_D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\epsilon\\ge0\\), and\n\\[\n\\|[G,L_x]\\|\\le\\epsilon\\|x\\|\n\\qquad\n(x\\in eLe,\\ e\\in D\\text{ a projection},\\ \\tau(e)=t).\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\nEqual-trace projections in \\(D\\) are unitarily conjugate, so, because \\(G\\) commutes with \\(L_D\\), it is equivalent to assume this estimate for one such projection.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(s=s^*=s^{-1}\\in A_0\\) and \\(\\tau(s)=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(c_0\\) is the constant in Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>With\n\\[\nc_1=6\\pi^2(1+4c_0),\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\left\\|\nP_{L^2(A_0)}[G,L_s]\\big|_{L^2(A_0)}\n\\right\\|\n\\le c_1\\epsilon.\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\nThe constant is independent of the trace \\(t\\) of the corner.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Lemma 3.2, replace \\(G\\) by its symmetrization. The original compressed commutator will be recovered exactly at the end.</p><p>Use the projections, operators, and identifications\n\\[\ne,p,d,w,u,U_k,\\mathcal K,\\iota,J\n\\]\nfrom Lemma 3.7, and \\(C=W^*(d,s)\\) from Lemma 3.8.</p><p>Let \\(f\\in L^\\infty(\\mathbb T,m)\\) be real, even, and of mean zero. Put\n\\[\nb_f=f(w)p,\\qquad h=eb_fe,\\qquad\n\\gamma_l=\\int_{\\mathbb T}z^lf(z)\\,dm(z)\n\\quad(l\\in\\mathbb Z).\n\\]\nBy Lemma 3.8, \\(b_f=b_f^*\\in pCp\\) and \\(\\tau_p(b_f)=0\\).</p><p>For \\(j,k\\ge0\\), test on \\(v,z\\in A_0\\). Since \\(eh=he=h\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\langle L_hU_kv,U_jz\\rangle\n&amp;=t^{-1}\\tau(z^*w^jhw^{-k}v)\\\\\n&amp;=\\left\\langle\nt^{-1}E_{A_0}(w^jhw^{-k})v,z\n\\right\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nDensity gives\n\\[\nU_j^*L_hU_k\n=\nL_{t^{-1}E_{A_0}(w^jhw^{-k})}\\big|_B.\n\\]</p><p>On \\(W^*(D,s)\\), the reduced-word decomposition shows that \\(E_{A_0}\\) equals the expectation onto\n\\[\nW^*(s)=\\mathbb C1+\\mathbb Cs:\n\\]\nevery reduced word containing a centered \\(D\\)-letter is orthogonal to \\(L^2(A_0)\\). Since \\(W^*(s)\\subset C\\), this expectation factors through \\(E_C\\). Lemma 3.9 and bimodularity give\n\\[\nE_C(w^jhw^{-k})=u^2w^jb_fw^{-k}.\n\\]</p><p>Using \\(dw^{-k}=w^kd\\) and \\(d=ws\\),\n\\[\nw^jpw^{-k}\n=\n\\frac{w^{j-k}+w^{j+k}d}{2}\n=\n\\frac{w^{j-k}+w^{j+k+1}s}{2}.\n\\]\nThe group trace gives\n\\[\nE_{W^*(s)}(f(w)w^l)=\\gamma_l1,\\qquad\nE_{W^*(s)}(f(w)w^ls)=\\gamma_ls,\n\\]\nfirst for Laurent polynomials, then by \\(L^2\\)-approximation. Therefore\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{-1}E_{A_0}(w^jhw^{-k})\n&amp;=\\frac{u^2}{2t}\n\\bigl(\\gamma_{j-k}1+\\gamma_{j+k+1}s\\bigr)\\\\\n&amp;=u\\bigl(\\gamma_{j-k}1+\\gamma_{j+k+1}s\\bigr),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\nU_j^*L_hU_k\n=\nu\\bigl(\\gamma_{j-k}I_B+\\gamma_{j+k+1}L_s|_B\\bigr).\n\\tag{32}\n\\]</p><p>The matrices\n\\[\nT_f=(\\gamma_{j-k})_{j,k\\ge0},\\qquad\nH_f=(\\gamma_{j+k+1})_{j,k\\ge0}\n\\]\nare bounded on \\(\\ell^2(\\mathbb N_0)\\), with norms at most \\(\\|f\\|_\\infty\\). Indeed, in the decomposition\n\\[\nL^2(\\mathbb T,m)=H_-\\oplus H_+,\n\\]\n\\[\nH_-=\\overline{\\operatorname{span}}\\{z^{-j}:j\\ge0\\},\\qquad\nH_+=\\overline{\\operatorname{span}}\\{z^{k+1}:k\\ge0\\},\n\\]\nthey are respectively the \\(H_-,H_-\\) and \\(H_-,H_+\\) blocks of multiplication by \\(f\\).</p><p>Equation (32), first on finite coordinate vectors and then by continuity, gives\n\\[\n\\iota^*L_h\\iota\n=\nu\\bigl(T_f\\otimes I_B+H_f\\otimes L_s|_B\\bigr).\n\\tag{33}\n\\]\nSince \\(J=I\\otimes B_0\\),\n\\[\n\\|[J,\\iota^*L_h\\iota]\\|\n=\nu\\|H_f\\|\\|[B_0,L_s|_B]\\|.\n\\tag{34}\n\\]\nHere the Hilbert-space tensor-product norm satisfies\n\\(\\|A\\otimes B\\|=\\|A\\|\\|B\\|\\).</p><p>Now set\n\\[\n\\theta=u/4=1/(2N),\\qquad 0&lt;\\theta\\le\\tfrac14,\n\\]\nand, for \\(z=e^{i\\alpha}\\), \\(-\\pi&lt;\\alpha\\le\\pi\\), choose\n\\[\nf(e^{i\\alpha})\n=\n1_{[-\\theta,\\theta]}(\\alpha)-\\frac{\\theta}{\\pi}.\n\\]\nThis function is real, even, and of mean zero. By Lemma 3.8,\n\\[\n\\|b_f\\|\\le1,\\qquad\n\\|b_f\\|_{2,\\tau_p}^2\n=\n\\frac{\\theta}{\\pi}\\left(1-\\frac{\\theta}{\\pi}\\right)\n\\le u.\n\\]\nLemma 3.9 gives\n\\[\n\\|h\\|=\\|eb_fe\\|\\le3u.\n\\tag{35}\n\\]</p><p>For \\(l\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n\\gamma_l=\\frac{\\sin(l\\theta)}{\\pi l}.\n\\]\nIf \\(0\\le j,k&lt;N\\), then\n\\[\n1\\le j+k+1\\le2N-1,\\qquad\n(j+k+1)\\theta&lt;1&lt;\\pi/2.\n\\]\nUsing \\(\\sin x\\ge2x/\\pi\\) on \\([0,\\pi/2]\\),\n\\[\n(H_f)_{jk}\\ge\\frac{2\\theta}{\\pi^2}.\n\\]\nTest the leading \\(N\\times N\\) block on its constant unit vector. Since \\(\\theta N=1/2\\),\n\\[\n\\|H_f\\|\\ge\\frac{2\\theta N}{\\pi^2}\n\\ge\\frac1{2\\pi^2}.\n\\tag{36}\n\\]</p><p>Combine (34), Lemma 3.7, (35), and (36):\n\\[\n\\frac{u}{2\\pi^2}\\|[B_0,L_s|_B]\\|\n\\le3u(1+4c_0)\\epsilon.\n\\]\nCancel \\(u&gt;0\\) to obtain\n\\[\n\\|[B_0,L_s|_B]\\|\n\\le6\\pi^2(1+4c_0)\\epsilon=c_1\\epsilon.\n\\]\nLemma 3.4 identifies this with the required compressed commutator for the symmetrized operator. Lemma 3.2 preserves that compression exactly, so the same estimate holds for the original \\(G\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( D'\\cap L=\\mathbb C1. \\) In particular, \\(L\\) is a \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor.", "statement": "<p>**Factoriality — original Lemma 3.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(D,A_0,L,\\tau\\) are as in Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">No assumption on \\(G\\) is needed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nD'\\cap L=\\mathbb C1.\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(L\\) is a \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\nE=L^2(D),\\qquad E^\\circ=E\\ominus\\mathbb C1,\\qquad\nB^\\circ=L^2(A_0)\\ominus\\mathbb C1.\n\\]\nRegrouping reduced words by their first and last \\(A_0\\)-letters gives the \\(D\\)-\\(D\\) bimodule decomposition\n\\[\nL^2(L)\\ominus L^2(D)\\cong E\\otimes V\\otimes E,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nV=\n\\bigoplus_{n\\ge1}\nB^\\circ\\otimes(E^\\circ\\otimes B^\\circ)^{\\otimes(n-1)}.\n\\]\nThe endpoint factors \\(E\\) are unrestricted. Expanding them as \\(\\mathbb C1\\oplus E^\\circ\\) recovers every reduced word containing an \\(A_0\\)-letter exactly once. Left and right \\(D\\) act on the first and last factors.</p><p>Choose a Haar unitary \\(v\\) in a diffuse abelian subalgebra of \\(D\\), and put \\(u_n=v^n\\). These unitaries converge ultraweakly to zero: the restriction of a normal functional to \\(W^*(v)\\cong L^\\infty(\\mathbb T)\\) has an \\(L^1(\\mathbb T)\\) density, whose Fourier coefficients tend to zero.</p><p>Consequently \\(L_{u_n}\\) and \\(R_{u_n^*}\\) converge weakly to zero on \\(E\\), first by testing against bounded vectors in \\(D\\), and then by \\(L^2\\)-density. On elementary tensors, the matrix coefficients of\n\\[\nL_{u_n}R_{u_n^*}\n=\nL_{u_n}\\otimes I_V\\otimes R_{u_n^*}\n\\]\ntend to zero. Density and uniform boundedness give the same conclusion on the whole complement.</p><p>A \\(D\\)-central vector in this complement is fixed by all these operators, hence is zero. If \\(z\\in D'\\cap L\\), its component perpendicular to \\(L^2(D)\\) is \\(D\\)-central. Therefore\n\\[\nz=E_D(z)\\in D,\n\\]\nwhere \\(E_D\\) is the trace-preserving conditional expectation. Thus \\(z\\in Z(D)=\\mathbb C1\\).</p><p>The center of \\(L\\) lies in \\(D'\\cap L\\). Its faithful finite trace and diffuse subfactor \\(D\\) make \\(L\\) a \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In proving Proposition 3, \\(G\\) may be replaced by an operator \\(\\widetilde G\\) that commutes with \\(W_u\\) for every \\( u\\in\\bigcup_j\\mathcal U(D_j). \\) The replacement:", "statement": "<p>**Symmetrization — original Lemma 3.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The assumptions of Proposition 3 hold.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(u\\in\\mathcal U(D)\\), let \\(\\theta_u\\) be the trace-preserving free-product automorphism equal to \\(\\operatorname{Ad}u\\) on \\(D\\) and the identity on \\(A_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W_u\\) be its Hilbert-space implementation. Thus\n\\[\nW_uL_xW_u^*=L_{\\theta_u(x)},\\qquad\nW_u\\xi=\\xi\\quad(\\xi\\in L^2(A_0)).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>In proving Proposition 3, \\(G\\) may be replaced by an operator \\(\\widetilde G\\) that commutes with \\(W_u\\) for every\n\\[\nu\\in\\bigcup_j\\mathcal U(D_j).\n\\]\nThe replacement:</p><p class=\"li\">still commutes with \\(L_D\\);</p><p class=\"li\">preserves the corner estimate for both \\(\\widetilde G\\) and \\(\\widetilde G^*\\);</p><p class=\"li\">preserves the compressed commutator exactly:\n\\[\nP_{L^2(A_0)}[\\widetilde G,L_s]\\big|_{L^2(A_0)}\n=\nP_{L^2(A_0)}[G,L_s]\\big|_{L^2(A_0)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Average over each compact matrix unitary group:\n\\[\nG_j=\\int_{\\mathcal U(D_j)}W_uGW_u^*\\,d\\mu_j(u).\n\\]\nThe weak operator integral is well defined. The representation \\(u\\mapsto W_u\\) is strongly continuous on each group, first on bounded reduced words and then by density.</p><p>Every conjugate commutes with \\(L_D\\). It also obeys the corner estimate, since \\(\\theta_u\\) permutes the \\(D\\)-corners of trace \\(t\\). Convexity and weak operator closedness of norm balls preserve these properties under integration and under a weak operator cluster limit \\(\\widetilde G\\) as \\(j\\to\\infty\\). Such a limit exists because\n\\[\n\\|G_j\\|\\le\\|G\\|.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(u\\in\\mathcal U(D_i)\\), every \\(G_j\\) with \\(j\\ge i\\) commutes with \\(W_u\\), by Haar invariance. Hence \\(\\widetilde G\\) has all the stated symmetries.</p><p>The operators \\(W_u\\) fix \\(L^2(A_0)\\) pointwise and commute with \\(L_s\\). Thus every conjugate, every average, and their cluster limit have the same compressed commutator:\n\\[\nP_{L^2(A_0)}[W_uGW_u^*,L_s]\\big|_{L^2(A_0)}\n=\nP_{L^2(A_0)}[G,L_s]\\big|_{L^2(A_0)}.\n\\]</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n[G^*,L_x]=-[G,L_{x^*}]^*.\n\\]\nThe hypothesis on a self-adjoint corner algebra therefore gives its adjoint version, and the same reasoning applies to \\(\\widetilde G\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\(k\\ne l\\), then \\(Y=0\\).", "statement": "<p>**Invariant intertwiners — original Lemma 3.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(D\\) and its increasing matrix subalgebras \\(D_j\\) are as in Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nE=L^2(D),\\qquad E^\\circ=E\\ominus\\mathbb C1.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(u\\in\\mathcal U(D)\\), write \\(C_u\\xi=u\\xi u^*\\), both on \\(E\\) and on \\(E^\\circ\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(k,l\\ge0\\), and\n\\[\nY:E\\otimes(E^\\circ)^{\\otimes k}\n\\longrightarrow E\\otimes(E^\\circ)^{\\otimes l}\n\\]\nis bounded.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(Y\\) intertwines the left \\(D\\)-actions on the first factors.</p><p class=\"li\">For every \\(u\\in\\bigcup_j\\mathcal U(D_j)\\),\n\\[\nYC_u^{\\otimes(k+1)}=C_u^{\\otimes(l+1)}Y.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(k\\ne l\\), then \\(Y=0\\). If \\(k=l\\), there are scalars \\((a_\\sigma)_{\\sigma\\in S_k}\\) such that\n\\[\nY=I_E\\otimes\\sum_{\\sigma\\in S_k}a_\\sigma U_\\sigma,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nU_\\sigma(x_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes x_k)\n=\nx_{\\sigma^{-1}(1)}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes x_{\\sigma^{-1}(k)}.\n\\]\nFor \\(k=0\\), the sum is the scalar multiple of the identity corresponding to the empty permutation.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(x\\in E\\), put \\(x^\\circ=x-\\tau(x)1\\). On unrestricted variables\n\\[\na,x_1,\\ldots,x_k,z_1,\\ldots,z_l\\in D,\n\\]\ndefine the complex multilinear form\n\\[\nF(a,x,z)=\n\\left\\langle\nY(1\\otimes x_1^\\circ\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes x_k^\\circ),\na^*\\otimes(z_1^*)^\\circ\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes(z_l^*)^\\circ\n\\right\\rangle.\n\\]</p><p>Fixing the tail vectors and slicing \\(Y\\) gives an operator \\(T:E\\to E\\) commuting with \\(L_D\\). The tracial commutation theorem gives \\(T=R_b\\) for \\(b\\in D\\), with\n\\[\n\\|b\\|=\\|T\\|\n\\le\\|Y\\|\\prod_i\\|x_i^\\circ\\|_2\n\\prod_j\\|(z_j^*)^\\circ\\|_2.\n\\]\nSince \\(F(a,x,z)=\\tau(ab)\\),\n\\[\n|F(a,x,z)|\n\\le\n\\|Y\\|\\|a\\|_1\n\\prod_{i=1}^k\\|x_i\\|_2\n\\prod_{j=1}^l\\|z_j\\|_2.\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\nThe same slice identifies coefficients with arbitrary bounded first endpoints: replacing \\(1,a^*\\) by \\(d,c\\) replaces \\(a\\) by \\(c^*d\\), since\n\\[\n\\langle db,c\\rangle=\\tau(c^*db).\n\\]</p><p>Put \\(p=1+k+l\\), and restrict all variables to a matrix stage \\(D_j\\cong M_m(\\mathbb C)\\). The intertwining assumption makes \\(F\\) invariant under simultaneous unitary conjugation. Label its variables \\(v_0,\\ldots,v_{p-1}\\). Every multilinear form has a unique expression\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}\\bigl(A(v_0\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_{p-1})\\bigr),\n\\qquad\nA\\in\\operatorname{End}((\\mathbb C^m)^{\\otimes p}),\n\\]\nby nondegeneracy of the trace pairing.</p><p>Conjugation invariance says that \\(A\\) commutes with all \\(u^{\\otimes p}\\). Finite-dimensional Schur–Weyl duality identifies this commutant with the span of tensor permutation operators; they are linearly independent when \\(m\\ge p\\) [10, Theorem 3.1]. Their trace pairings give the cycle-trace forms\n\\[\n\\Phi_\\pi(v_0,\\ldots,v_{p-1})\n=\n\\prod_{\\substack{(i_1\\cdots i_r)\\\\\\text{a cycle of }\\pi}}\n\\tau(v_{i_1}\\cdots v_{i_r}),\n\\qquad \\pi\\in S_p.\n\\]\nThe normalization from \\(\\operatorname{Tr}\\) to \\(\\tau\\) multiplies each form by a nonzero scalar, so these forms are also independent for \\(m\\ge p\\). Therefore\n\\[\nF\\big|_{D_j^p}=\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_p}\\lambda_{\\pi,j}\\Phi_\\pi.\n\\]</p><p>This identity is on unrestricted matrix variables: centering occurs inside the definition of \\(F\\). Normalized traces agree under unital inclusions of matrix stages. Hence each \\(\\Phi_\\pi\\) restricts to the same form at the smaller stage, and uniqueness at any smaller stage of size at least \\(p\\) shows that \\(\\lambda_{\\pi,j}\\) is eventually independent of \\(j\\). Denote the stable coefficient by \\(\\lambda_\\pi\\).</p><p>Fix \\(\\pi\\in S_p\\). Choose distinct matrix indices for the slots and put\n\\[\nv_i=e_{i,\\pi(i)}.\n\\]\nA nonzero product must follow the successors prescribed by \\(\\pi\\), so\n\\[\n\\Phi_\\rho(v_0,\\ldots,v_{p-1})\n=\n\\begin{cases}\nm^{-c(\\pi)},&amp;\\rho=\\pi,\\\\\n0,&amp;\\rho\\ne\\pi,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nwhere \\(c(\\pi)\\) is the number of cycles. Every chosen matrix unit has \\(L^1\\)-norm \\(m^{-1}\\) and \\(L^2\\)-norm \\(m^{-1/2}\\), including diagonal units. The endpoint estimate gives\n\\[\n|\\lambda_\\pi|m^{-c(\\pi)}\n\\le\\|Y\\|m^{-1-(k+l)/2}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\lambda_\\pi\\) is fixed while the stage size tends to infinity,\n\\[\n\\lambda_\\pi=0\n\\quad\\text{if}\\quad\nc(\\pi)&lt;1+\\frac{k+l}{2}.\n\\]</p><p>Now restrict the tail variables to centered matrices. A singleton tail cycle makes its form vanish. If the cycle containing \\(a=v_0\\) has length \\(r\\), a permutation without singleton tail cycles has at most\n\\[\n1+\\frac{k+l-(r-1)}2\n\\]\ncycles. When \\(r&gt;1\\), this is below the required threshold. When \\(r=1\\), the threshold is attained only if every tail cycle has length two. Thus, on centered tails,\n\\[\nF(a,x,z)\n=\n\\tau(a)\\sum_P\\lambda_P\\prod_{\\{i,j\\}\\in P}\\tau(v_iv_j),\n\\]\nwhere \\(P\\) ranges over pairings of the tail slots. There are no terms when the number of tail slots is odd.</p><p>Using \\(a=c^*d\\) gives the coefficients with first endpoints \\(d,c\\) in the union of the matrix stages. These formulas extend to arbitrary endpoints in \\(E\\) and centered \\(L^2\\)-tail letters: the union is \\(L^2\\)-dense, the left side is continuous in its individual \\(L^2\\)-letters, and\n\\[\n|\\tau(uv)|\\le\\|u\\|_2\\|v\\|_2.\n\\]\nAt this point the assertion concerns coefficients on simple tensors, not boundedness of each pairing on the completed tensor product.</p><p>Choose an infinite orthonormal family \\((h_n)\\) of self-adjoint centered vectors in \\(E\\), for instance normalized real trigonometric functions of a Haar unitary. Then\n\\[\n\\tau(h_nh_m)=\\delta_{nm}.\n\\]\nFix a pairing \\(P\\), with \\(r_s\\) pairs entirely in source slots and \\(r_t\\) pairs entirely in test slots. Assign a disjoint infinite pool of the \\(h_n\\) to each pair.</p><p>For each source–test pair, use one fixed member of its pool in both slots. For each source–source pair, sum the source tensor over \\(M\\) members of its pool, placing the same member in its two slots, independently for the different pairs. Form the test tensor analogously for test–test pairs. Take both first endpoints to be \\(1\\).</p><p>In each expanded term, every label occurs exactly twice in the positions assigned by \\(P\\), and different pairs have different labels. All other pairings therefore contribute zero. The coefficient is\n\\[\n\\lambda_PM^{r_s+r_t}.\n\\]\nThe source summands are orthonormal, as are the test summands, so the product of their norms is \\(M^{(r_s+r_t)/2}\\). Boundedness of \\(Y\\) gives\n\\[\n|\\lambda_P|M^{r_s+r_t}\n\\le\\|Y\\|M^{(r_s+r_t)/2}.\n\\]\nLetting \\(M\\to\\infty\\) proves \\(\\lambda_P=0\\) if \\(r_s+r_t&gt;0\\), including when one side has no tail slots.</p><p>Every surviving pair joins a source slot to a test slot. This requires \\(k=l\\). Otherwise all simple-tensor coefficients vanish and \\(Y=0\\). When \\(k=l\\), the surviving pairings are indexed by bijections \\(\\sigma\\in S_k\\), with coefficients\n\\[\n\\tau(c^*d)\\sum_{\\sigma\\in S_k}a_\\sigma\n\\prod_{i=1}^k\\tau(x_i z_{\\sigma(i)}).\n\\]\nSince \\(\\tau(x_i z_j)=\\langle x_i,z_j^*\\rangle\\), these are precisely the coefficients of\n\\[\nI_E\\otimes\\sum_{\\sigma\\in S_k}a_\\sigma U_\\sigma.\n\\]\nEquality against all elementary test tensors and their density prove the operator identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every \\(H_k\\) reduces \\(G\\).", "statement": "<p>**Word structure — original Lemma 3.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The setting of Proposition 3 holds.</p><p class=\"li\">In addition, \\(G\\) has been symmetrized as in Lemma 3.2, so it commutes with every \\(W_u\\), \\(u\\in\\bigcup_j\\mathcal U(D_j)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\nE=L^2(D),\\quad E^\\circ=E\\ominus\\mathbb C1,\\quad\nB=L^2(A_0),\\quad B^\\circ=B\\ominus\\mathbb C1,\n\\]\n\\[\nJ_k=(B^\\circ)^{\\otimes k}\\otimes B,\\qquad\nH_k=E\\otimes(E^\\circ)^{\\otimes k}\\otimes J_k.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the reduced-word identification\n\\[\nL^2(L)=\\bigoplus_{k\\ge0}H_k,\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\nunder which\n\\[\nd_0\\otimes d_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes d_k\n\\otimes(a_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes a_k\\otimes z)\n\\]\nrepresents\n\\[\nd_0a_1d_1\\cdots a_kd_kz.\n\\]\nInitially \\(d_i\\in D\\ominus\\mathbb C1\\) and \\(a_i\\in A_0\\ominus\\mathbb C1\\) for \\(i\\ge1\\), while \\(d_0\\in D\\) and \\(z\\in A_0\\) are unrestricted. Expanding the endpoints into scalar and centered parts gives all reduced-word spaces exactly once.</p><p class=\"li\">Left \\(D\\) acts on the first factor, while \\(W_u\\) conjugates all \\(k+1\\) factors from \\(D\\) and fixes \\(J_k\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\(H_k\\) reduces \\(G\\). There are \\(B_k\\in B(J_k)\\), with\n\\[\n\\|B_k\\|\\le\\|G\\|,\n\\]\nsuch that, for all \\(d_0\\in E\\), \\(x\\in E^\\circ\\), and \\(\\alpha\\in J_k\\),\n\\[\nG(d_0\\otimes x^{\\otimes k}\\otimes\\alpha)\n=\nd_0\\otimes x^{\\otimes k}\\otimes B_k\\alpha,\n\\]\n\\[\nG^*(d_0\\otimes x^{\\otimes k}\\otimes\\alpha)\n=\nd_0\\otimes x^{\\otimes k}\\otimes B_k^*\\alpha.\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\nThese are identities in the full space \\(L^2(L)\\). The operators \\(B_k\\) are independent of the repeated letter \\(x\\), which may be any complex centered \\(L^2\\)-vector.</p><p>At level zero,\n\\[\nG|_{H_0}=I_E\\otimes B_0,\\qquad\n\\left\\|P_B[G,L_s]\\big|_B\\right\\|\n=\n\\|[B_0,L_s|_B]\\|.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(P_k\\) be the level projection. It commutes with \\(L_D\\) and all \\(W_u\\). For \\(\\alpha\\in J_k\\) and \\(\\beta\\in J_l\\), slice \\(P_lGP_k\\) in these factors to obtain\n\\[\nY_{\\alpha,\\beta}:\nE\\otimes(E^\\circ)^{\\otimes k}\n\\to E\\otimes(E^\\circ)^{\\otimes l},\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n\\|Y_{\\alpha,\\beta}\\|\\le\\|G\\|\\|\\alpha\\|\\|\\beta\\|.\n\\]\nIt satisfies Lemma 3.3. For \\(k\\ne l\\), every slice vanishes, so \\(P_lGP_k=0\\). Hence the levels reduce \\(G\\).</p><p>Fix a unit vector \\(h\\in E^\\circ\\), and define the isometry\n\\[\nV_k:J_k\\to H_k,\\qquad\nV_k\\alpha=1\\otimes h^{\\otimes k}\\otimes\\alpha.\n\\]\nPut \\(B_k=V_k^*GV_k\\). On an equal-level slice, Lemma 3.3 gives\n\\[\nY_{\\alpha,\\beta}\n=\nI_E\\otimes\\sum_{\\sigma\\in S_k}a_\\sigma(\\alpha,\\beta)U_\\sigma,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\langle B_k\\alpha,\\beta\\rangle\n=\n\\sum_{\\sigma\\in S_k}a_\\sigma(\\alpha,\\beta).\n\\]\nEvery \\(U_\\sigma\\) fixes \\(x^{\\otimes k}\\) for every \\(x\\in E^\\circ\\). Thus the scalar is independent of the unit letter used to define \\(V_k\\).</p><p>Test the same slice identity against arbitrary endpoint and tail vectors and arbitrary \\(\\beta\\). This gives the asserted full-output identity for \\(G\\). These tests determine the output on level \\(k\\), and all other levels have already been excluded. The endpoint \\(d_0\\) can be any vector in \\(E\\); no bounded representative is required. No self-adjointness of \\(x\\) is used, since the cross contraction is\n\\[\n\\tau(xz)=\\langle x,z^*\\rangle.\n\\]</p><p>Apply the same argument to \\(G^*\\). Its induced operator is\n\\[\nV_k^*G^*V_k=(V_k^*GV_k)^*=B_k^*.\n\\]\nAt level zero \\(B\\) is the subspace \\(\\mathbb C1\\otimes B\\), giving the final commutator identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every projection \\(r\\in L\\) with \\(\\tau(r)\\le t\\), there is \\(b\\in L\\), satisfying \\(b=rb\\), such that \\( \\|(G-R_b)|_{Hr}\\|\\le c_0\\epsilon. \\) The same assertion holds for \\(G^*\\).", "statement": "<p>**Full columns from a small corner — original Lemma 4.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The assumptions of Proposition 3 hold.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(H=L^2(L)\\), and \\(Hr\\) denotes the range of right multiplication by a projection \\(r\\in L\\).</p><p class=\"li\">This assertion uses only the corner estimate and exact commutation with \\(L_D\\); the additional symmetries of Lemma 3.2 are not required.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every projection \\(r\\in L\\) with \\(\\tau(r)\\le t\\), there is \\(b\\in L\\), satisfying \\(b=rb\\), such that\n\\[\n\\|(G-R_b)|_{Hr}\\|\\le c_0\\epsilon.\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\nThe same assertion holds for \\(G^*\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The case \\(r=0\\) is immediate. Fix \\(r\\ne0\\) and a projection \\(e\\in D\\) with \\(\\tau(e)=t\\).</p><p>By Lemma 3.1, \\(L\\) is a factor. Projection comparison gives \\(r'\\le e\\) and \\(v\\in L\\) with\n\\[\nv^*v=r,\\qquad vv^*=r'.\n\\]\nRight multiplication by \\(v^*\\) is a unitary left \\(eLe\\)-module map\n\\[\neHr\\longrightarrow eHr',\\qquad \\xi\\longmapsto\\xi v^*.\n\\]\nIts inverse is right multiplication by \\(v\\), and\n\\[\n\\|\\xi v^*\\|_2^2\n=\\tau(v\\xi^*\\xi v^*)\n=\\tau(\\xi^*\\xi r)\n=\\|\\xi\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nThese equalities extend to \\(L^2\\)-vectors by continuity.</p><p>The vector \\(r'\\) is cyclic for the left action of \\(eLe\\) on \\(eHr'\\), since\n\\[\n(eLe)r'=eLr'\n\\]\nis dense there. Hence \\(eHr\\) is a cyclic domain representation of the unital algebra \\(eLe\\).</p><p>Because \\(G\\) commutes with \\(L_e\\), its restriction gives\n\\[\nG_e:eHr\\to eH.\n\\]\nThe map\n\\[\n\\Delta(a)=G_eL_a|_{eHr}-L_aG_e\n\\qquad(a\\in eLe)\n\\]\nis a rectangular derivation of norm at most \\(\\epsilon\\). Proposition 1 supplies an exact intertwiner \\(T:eHr\\to eH\\) with\n\\[\nTL_a|_{eHr}=L_aT,\\qquad\n\\|G_e-T\\|\\le c_0\\epsilon.\n\\]</p><p>Since \\(t=1/N\\), choose \\(v_1,\\ldots,v_N\\in D\\) with\n\\[\nv_i^*v_i=e,\\qquad\nv_iv_i^*\\perp v_jv_j^*\\ (i\\ne j),\\qquad\n\\sum_{i=1}^Nv_iv_i^*=1.\n\\]\nThe maps\n\\[\n(\\xi_i)\\longmapsto\\sum_i v_i\\xi_i\n\\]\nidentify \\((eHr)^N\\) with \\(Hr\\), and \\((eH)^N\\) with \\(H\\), unitarily. Define\n\\[\n\\widetilde T\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^Nv_i\\xi_i\\right)\n=\n\\sum_{i=1}^Nv_iT\\xi_i.\n\\]\nIn these coordinates \\(\\widetilde T=T^{(N)}\\), while \\(G|_{Hr}=G_e^{(N)}\\), since \\(G\\) commutes with every \\(L_{v_i}\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\|G|_{Hr}-\\widetilde T\\|\n=\\|G_e-T\\|\\le c_0\\epsilon.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(a\\in L\\), its left action has matrix entries \\(L_{v_i^*av_j}\\), each from \\(eLe\\). The intertwining identity for \\(T\\) gives\n\\[\n\\widetilde TL_a|_{Hr}=L_a\\widetilde T.\n\\]\nExtend \\(\\widetilde T\\) by zero on \\(H(1-r)\\). Since \\(R_r\\) commutes with all left multiplications, this extension belongs to \\(L(L)'\\), hence is \\(R_b\\) for a bounded \\(b\\in L\\). Vanishing on \\(H(1-r)\\) means\n\\[\nR_b=R_bR_r,\n\\]\nwhich, under \\(R_b\\xi=\\xi b\\), says \\(b=rb\\).</p><p>Finally \\(G^*\\) commutes with \\(L_D\\) and\n\\[\n[G^*,L_a]=-[G,L_{a^*}]^*.\n\\]\nThus the same argument applies to \\(G^*\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(k\\ge1\\) and \\(v\\in B\\), \\( \\|B_k(s^{\\otimes k}\\otimes v)-s^{\\otimes k}\\otimes B_0v\\| \\le2c_0\\epsilon\\|v\\|_2. \\) The same estimate holds with \\(B_k^*,B_0^*\\) in place of \\(B_k,B_0\\).", "statement": "<p>**Propagation along repeated letters — original Lemma 4.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The assumptions of Proposition 3 hold.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(G\\) is symmetrized as in Lemma 3.2.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(B=L^2(A_0)\\), \\(J_k=(B^\\circ)^{\\otimes k}\\otimes B\\), and \\(B_k\\in B(J_k)\\) are the operators of Lemma 3.4.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(s=s^*=s^{-1}\\in A_0\\) has trace zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(k\\ge1\\) and \\(v\\in B\\),\n\\[\n\\|B_k(s^{\\otimes k}\\otimes v)-s^{\\otimes k}\\otimes B_0v\\|\n\\le2c_0\\epsilon\\|v\\|_2.\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\nThe same estimate holds with \\(B_k^*,B_0^*\\) in place of \\(B_k,B_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(k\\ge1\\), and first take \\(v\\in A_0\\); the zero vector is harmless. Choose a nonzero projection \\(q\\in D\\), with\n\\[\na=\\tau(q)\\le t.\n\\]\nThe left support of \\(qv\\) is dominated by \\(q\\). Its right support \\(r\\) is equivalent to its left support, so\n\\[\n\\tau(r)\\le a\\le t.\n\\]\nChoose \\(b=rb\\) from Lemma 3.5, and put\n\\[\ny=qvb-qB_0v.\n\\]\nLemma 3.4 at level zero gives \\(G(qv)=qB_0v\\), while the tensor decomposition gives\n\\[\n\\|qv\\|_2=\\sqrt a\\,\\|v\\|_2.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nqy=y,\\qquad\n\\|y\\|_2\\le c_0\\epsilon\\sqrt a\\,\\|v\\|_2.\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\nThe vector \\(B_0v\\) need only lie in \\(L^2(A_0)\\); all expressions use bounded left multiplication on it.</p><p>Let \\(z\\) be a Haar unitary of \\(qDq\\) for its normalized trace, and set\n\\[\nx_n=a^{-1/2}z^n\\qquad(n\\ge1).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\tau(x_n)=0,\\quad \\|x_n\\|_2=1,\\quad\nx_n^*x_n=x_nx_n^*=a^{-1}q,\n\\]\nand \\(x_n,x_n^*\\rightharpoonup0\\) in \\(L^2(D)\\), by orthonormality of the positive and negative powers.</p><p>The reduced words\n\\[\n\\xi_n=(sx_n)^kv\n\\]\nhave norm \\(\\|v\\|_2\\) and right support at most \\(r\\), because the last \\(x_n\\) satisfies \\(x_nq=x_n\\). Put\n\\[\nA_n=(sx_n)^{k-1}s.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\xi_nb-(sx_n)^kB_0v=A_nx_ny.\n\\]\nLemma 3.5 gives\n\\[\n\\|G\\xi_n-(sx_n)^kB_0v\\|_2\n\\le c_0\\epsilon\\|v\\|_2+\\|A_nx_ny\\|_2.\n\\tag{24}\n\\]</p><p>Write the decomposition in Lemma 3.4 as\n\\[\nH=E\\otimes F,\\qquad\nF=\\bigoplus_{l\\ge0}(E^\\circ)^{\\otimes l}\\otimes J_l,\n\\]\nand let \\(P_{\\mathrm{sc}}\\) project onto \\(\\mathbb C1\\otimes F\\). It keeps every word level and selects the scalar component of its first \\(D\\)-factor. Left multiplication by \\(x_n\\) acts on \\(E\\).</p><p>Define\n\\[\n\\ell_n(\\eta)=\\langle\\eta,x_n^*\\rangle.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nP_{\\mathrm{sc}}(x_ny)=1\\otimes(\\ell_n\\otimes I_F)y,\n\\qquad\n\\|\\ell_n\\|=1.\n\\]\nThis tends to zero in norm on elementary tensors because \\(x_n^*\\rightharpoonup0\\). Approximation of the fixed vector \\(y\\) by finite tensor sums, using \\(\\|\\ell_n\\|=1\\), gives\n\\[\n\\|P_{\\mathrm{sc}}(x_ny)\\|_2\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{25}\n\\]</p><p>On \\((1-P_{\\mathrm{sc}})H\\), multiplication by \\(A_n\\) prepends a reduced centered word ending in \\(s\\) to a leading centered \\(D\\)-letter. Each prefix letter has \\(L^2\\)-norm one, so this restriction is an isometry. On \\(P_{\\mathrm{sc}}H\\), use\n\\[\n\\|L_{A_n}\\|\\le a^{-(k-1)/2}.\n\\]\nAlso \\(qy=y\\) gives\n\\[\n\\|x_ny\\|_2^2\n=\\langle L_{x_n^*x_n}y,y\\rangle\n=a^{-1}\\|y\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\|A_nx_ny\\|_2\n\\le\n\\|(1-P_{\\mathrm{sc}})x_ny\\|_2\n+a^{-(k-1)/2}\\|P_{\\mathrm{sc}}x_ny\\|_2,\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n\\limsup_n\\|A_nx_ny\\|_2\n\\le a^{-1/2}\\|y\\|_2\n\\le c_0\\epsilon\\|v\\|_2.\n\\]\nHere \\(q,k,v,b,y\\) are fixed before \\(n\\to\\infty\\). Thus the possibly large prefix bound disappears with the vanishing coefficient; no convergence rate uniform in \\(a\\) or \\(k\\) is needed.</p><p>By the full-output identity of Lemma 3.4, the left side of (24) equals\n\\[\n\\|B_k(s^{\\otimes k}\\otimes v)-s^{\\otimes k}\\otimes B_0v\\|\n\\]\nfor every \\(n\\), since its endpoint and repeated \\(D\\)-factors have norm one. Taking the limit superior proves the estimate for bounded \\(v\\). Density and boundedness extend it to all \\(v\\in B\\).</p><p>Apply the same argument to \\(G^*\\), using the adjoint identities in Lemma 3.4, to obtain the final assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.7", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(h\\in eLe\\), \\( \\|[J,\\iota^*L_h\\iota]\\| \\le(1+4c_0)\\epsilon\\|h\\|. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Compression of the commutator — original Lemma 4.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The assumptions of Proposition 3 hold, and \\(G\\) is symmetrized.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose projections \\(e,p\\in D\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\tau(e)=t,\\qquad e\\le p,\\qquad \\tau(p)=\\tfrac12.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n\\[\nd=2p-1,\\qquad w=ds,\\qquad\n\\tau_p=2\\tau|_{pLp},\\qquad u=\\tau_p(e)=2t.\n\\tag{26}\n\\]\nThe elements \\(d,s\\) are free centered symmetries.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(B=L^2(A_0)\\), and define\n\\[\nU_k:B\\to eH,\\qquad\nU_kv=t^{-1/2}ew^{-k}v=t^{-1/2}e(sd)^kv\n\\qquad(k\\ge0).\n\\]\nIn the word decomposition, this tensor has endpoint \\(t^{-1/2}e\\), repeated \\(D\\)-letter \\(d\\), and last factor \\(s^{\\otimes k}\\otimes v\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The \\(U_k\\) are isometries with mutually orthogonal ranges in the respective levels \\(H_k\\). Define\n\\[\n\\mathcal K=\\bigoplus_{k\\ge0}U_kB\\subset eH,\n\\]\nidentify \\(\\mathcal K\\cong\\ell^2(\\mathbb N_0)\\otimes B\\), and let\n\\[\nJ=I\\otimes B_0,\\qquad\n\\iota:\\mathcal K\\hookrightarrow H\n\\]\nbe the inclusion.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(h\\in eLe\\),\n\\[\n\\|[J,\\iota^*L_h\\iota]\\|\n\\le(1+4c_0)\\epsilon\\|h\\|.\n\\tag{27}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 3.4 and 3.6 give\n\\[\n\\|GU_kv-U_kB_0v\\|_2\\le2c_0\\epsilon\\|v\\|_2,\n\\]\nwith zero error for \\(k=0\\). Each error lies in level \\(k\\), so squared norms add on finite orthogonal sums. Passing to the closure, and doing the same for \\(G^*\\), gives the full-codomain estimates\n\\[\n\\|G\\iota-\\iota J\\|\\le2c_0\\epsilon,\\qquad\n\\|G^*\\iota-\\iota J^*\\|\\le2c_0\\epsilon.\n\\tag{28}\n\\]</p><p>Write\n\\[\nR=G\\iota-\\iota J,\\qquad\nR'=G^*\\iota-\\iota J^*,\\qquad\nT_h=\\iota^*L_h\\iota.\n\\]\nTaking the adjoint of the second error identity gives\n\\[\n\\iota^*G=J\\iota^*+(R')^*.\n\\]\nA direct expansion yields\n\\[\n\\iota^*[G,L_h]\\iota\n=\n[J,T_h]+(R')^*L_h\\iota-\\iota^*L_hR.\n\\]\nThe corner estimate bounds the compressed commutator by \\(\\epsilon\\|h\\|\\). Each error term has norm at most \\(2c_0\\epsilon\\|h\\|\\). Rearranging proves the claim.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.8", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( pCp= \\{f(w)p:f\\in L^\\infty(\\mathbb T,m),\\ f(z)=f(z^{-1})\\}, \\) and \\( \\tau_p(f(w)p)=\\int_{\\mathbb T}f\\,dm. \\) The map \\(f\\mapsto f(w)p\\) is a faithful normal unital \\(*\\)-isomorphism from the even-function algebra …", "statement": "<p>**The abelian dihedral corner and corner freeness — original Lemma 5.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(L=D*A_0\\) is as in Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(p\\in D\\) is a projection of trace \\(1/2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(s=s^*=s^{-1}\\in A_0\\) has trace zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nd=2p-1,\\qquad w=ds,\\qquad C=W^*(d,s),\\qquad\n\\tau_p=2\\tau|_{pLp}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(m\\) is Haar probability measure on \\(\\mathbb T\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Freeness of \\(d,s\\) identifies their alternating reduced words with the group words of the infinite dihedral group. In particular,\n\\[\nw\\text{ is Haar},\\qquad dwd=w^{-1},\\qquad p=(1+d)/2.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\npCp=\n\\{f(w)p:f\\in L^\\infty(\\mathbb T,m),\\ f(z)=f(z^{-1})\\},\n\\tag{29}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\tau_p(f(w)p)=\\int_{\\mathbb T}f\\,dm.\n\\]\nThe map \\(f\\mapsto f(w)p\\) is a faithful normal unital \\(*\\)-isomorphism from the even-function algebra onto \\(pCp\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n\\|f(w)p\\|=\\|f\\|_\\infty,\\qquad\n\\|f(w)p\\|_{2,\\tau_p}=\\|f\\|_{L^2(m)}.\n\\]\nThe algebras \\(pDp\\) and \\(pCp\\) are free with respect to \\(\\tau_p\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Every group word has the form \\(w^l\\) or \\(w^ld\\), \\(l\\in\\mathbb Z\\). Since \\(dp=p\\) and \\(dw^ld=w^{-l}\\),\n\\[\npw^ldp=pw^lp=\\frac{w^l+w^{-l}}2p.\n\\]\nAn even function of \\(w\\) commutes with \\(d\\), hence with \\(p\\). Thus \\(f\\mapsto f(w)p\\) is a normal \\(*\\)-homomorphism on the even-function algebra, with unit \\(p\\).</p><p>The group trace gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tau_p(f(w)p)\n&amp;=2\\tau(f(w)p)\\\\\n&amp;=\\tau(f(w))+\\tau(f(w)d)\n=\\int_{\\mathbb T}f\\,dm,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nfirst for trigonometric polynomials, then for bounded functions by \\(L^2\\)-approximation. This makes the map faithful, with von Neumann algebra range. The compressed group-word span is strongly dense in \\(pCp\\) and consists of even Laurent polynomials times \\(p\\). This proves the identification and the norm assertions.</p><p>For freeness, let \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_j\\in pDp\\) be \\(\\tau_p\\)-centered. Then\n\\[\n\\tau(a_i)=0,\\qquad a_id=a_i.\n\\]\nThe centered trigonometric span in \\(pCp\\) is generated by \\(pw^lp\\), \\(l\\ge1\\). For \\(l_1,\\ldots,l_j\\ge1\\), absorbing corner projections and using traciality gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tau_p(a_1pw^{l_1}p\\cdots a_jpw^{l_j}p)\n&amp;=2\\tau(a_1w^{l_1}\\cdots a_jw^{l_j})\\\\\n&amp;=2\\tau\\bigl(a_1s(ds)^{l_1-1}\\cdots\na_js(ds)^{l_j-1}\\bigr)=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAll displayed \\(D\\)-letters are centered, and \\(s\\) is centered and free from the whole algebra \\(D\\), so the last expression is the trace of a reduced alternating word.</p><p>Fejér means of an even bounded zero-mean function are even zero-mean trigonometric polynomials, are uniformly bounded, and converge in \\(L^2(m)\\). Approximation of each corner letter extends the vanishing to all cyclic alternating products of even length.</p><p>For odd alternating length, the endpoints lie in the same algebra. Move one endpoint cyclically next to the other, split their product into centered and scalar parts, and apply the even-length case and induction on the length. The length-one case is centeredness. This proves freeness.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.9", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( E_C(ebe)=u^2b, \\) and \\( \\|ebe\\| \\le2\\sqrt u\\,\\|b\\|_{2,\\tau_p}+u\\|b\\|. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Free compression — original Lemma 5.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(L=D*A_0\\), \\(p\\), \\(d=2p-1\\), \\(s\\), \\(C=W^*(d,s)\\), and \\(\\tau_p=2\\tau|_{pLp}\\) are as in Lemma 3.8.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(e\\in D\\) is a projection with \\(e\\le p\\), and \\(u=\\tau_p(e)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(E_C:L\\to C\\) is the trace-preserving conditional expectation.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(b=b^*\\in pCp\\) and \\(\\tau_p(b)=0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nE_C(ebe)=u^2b,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\|ebe\\|\n\\le2\\sqrt u\\,\\|b\\|_{2,\\tau_p}+u\\|b\\|.\n\\tag{30}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\nA=pDp,\\qquad Z=pCp,\\qquad a=e-up\\in A.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\tau_p(a)=0\\). Since \\(p\\in C\\), bimodularity shows that \\(E_C(ebe)\\in Z\\). On \\(pLp\\), \\(E_C\\) restricts to the \\(\\tau_p\\)-preserving expectation onto \\(Z\\).</p><p>Expand\n\\[\nebe=u^2b+u(ab+ba)+aba.\n\\]\nBy Lemma 3.8, \\(A\\) and \\(Z\\) are free. Thus \\(ab\\) and \\(ba\\) have zero expectation onto \\(Z\\). For \\(aba\\), test against \\(c\\in Z\\) and write\n\\[\nc=c^\\circ+\\tau_p(c)p.\n\\]\nFreeness and traciality give\n\\[\n\\tau_p(c^\\circ aba)=0,\\qquad\n\\tau_p(aba)=\\tau_p(ba^2)=\\tau_p(b)\\tau_p(a^2)=0.\n\\]\nTherefore \\(E_Z(aba)=0\\), proving the expectation formula.</p><p>For the norm estimate, work in the faithful representation\n\\[\nH_c=L^2(W^*(A,Z),\\tau_p).\n\\]\nGrouping a possible initial \\(A\\)-letter gives the free-word decomposition\n\\[\nH_c=L^2(A,\\tau_p)\\otimes F_0,\\qquad\nF_0=\\mathbb C\\Omega\\oplus F_0^\\circ,\n\\]\nwhere \\(F_0^\\circ\\) is the closed span of reduced words starting in the centered \\(L^2\\)-subspace \\(Z^\\circ\\).</p><p>Let \\(Q_0\\) project onto the complement of the vacuum in \\(F_0\\), and let \\(\\Pi\\) project in \\(H_c\\) onto words starting in \\(Z^\\circ\\). Then\n\\[\n\\Pi=|1_A\\rangle\\langle1_A|\\otimes Q_0,\\qquad\nL_e=\\lambda_A(e)\\otimes I_{F_0},\n\\]\nwhere \\(1_A=p\\) has \\(L^2(\\tau_p)\\)-norm one. Consequently\n\\[\n\\|L_e\\Pi\\|=\\|\\Pi L_e\\|\n\\le\\|e\\|_{2,\\tau_p}=\\sqrt u.\n\\tag{31}\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(T=L_b\\). Since \\(b\\) is centered, its action on the vacuum and on words starting in \\(A^\\circ\\) simply prepends \\(b\\). Hence\n\\[\n(1-\\Pi)T(1-\\Pi)=0,\\qquad\n\\|\\Pi T(1-\\Pi)\\|\\le\\|b\\|_{2,\\tau_p}.\n\\]\nSelf-adjointness gives the same estimate for \\((1-\\Pi)T\\Pi\\), while\n\\[\n\\|\\Pi T\\Pi\\|\\le\\|b\\|.\n\\]\nThese are the creation, annihilation, and diagonal blocks of a centered free-product letter, as in [26, Section 1].</p><p>Decomposing \\(T\\) into these blocks and using \\(\\|L_e\\|\\le1\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|ebe\\|=\\|L_eTL_e\\|\n&amp;\\le\n\\|L_e\\Pi T(1-\\Pi)L_e\\|\n+\\|L_e(1-\\Pi)T\\Pi L_e\\|\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad+\\|L_e\\Pi T\\Pi L_e\\|\\\\\n&amp;\\le\n\\sqrt u\\,\\|b\\|_{2,\\tau_p}\n+\\sqrt u\\,\\|b\\|_{2,\\tau_p}\n+u\\|b\\|.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a unital subfactor \\(R\\subset M\\) satisfying \\( R'\\cap M=\\mathbb C1, \\) such that \\(R\\) is generated by an increasing sequence of unital full matrix algebras whose sizes tend to infinity.", "statement": "<p>**An irreducible increasing-matrix subfactor — original Lemma 6.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(M\\) is a separable-predual \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor, with normalized trace \\(\\tau\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a unital subfactor \\(R\\subset M\\) satisfying\n\\[\nR'\\cap M=\\mathbb C1,\n\\]\nsuch that \\(R\\) is generated by an increasing sequence of unital full matrix algebras whose sizes tend to infinity.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a countable \\(L^2\\)-dense subset\n\\[\n\\{x_j:j\\ge1\\}\n\\]\nof the self-adjoint unit ball of \\(M\\), and enumerate all requirements\n\\[\n(j,k)\\in\\mathbb N^2.\n\\]\nStart with \\(F_0=\\mathbb C1\\).</p><p>If the current stage is a unital full matrix algebra \\(F_n\\), put\n\\[\nN_n=F_n'\\cap M.\n\\]\nThis is a \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor: matrix units identify \\(M\\) with a matrix algebra over a corner and \\(N_n\\) with that corner.</p><p>For the next requirement \\((j,k)\\), apply Lemma 4.1 inside \\(N_n\\) to\n\\[\ny=E_{N_n}(x_j)\n\\]\nwith error less than \\(1/k\\). Obtain a full matrix algebra \\(F\\subset N_n\\), and set\n\\[\nF_{n+1}=F_n\\vee F.\n\\]\nIt is a full matrix algebra of size at least twice that of \\(F_n\\), and\n\\[\nN_{n+1}=F'\\cap N_n.\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\nE_{N_{n+1}}=E_{N_{n+1}}E_{N_n},\n\\]\none obtains\n\\[\n\\|E_{N_{n+1}}(x_j)-\\tau(x_j)1\\|_2&lt;1/k.\n\\]\nAll previously obtained bounds persist because the relative commutants decrease and their trace expectations compose.</p><p>Let\n\\[\nR=\\left(\\bigcup_nF_n\\right)'',\\qquad\nN=R'\\cap M=\\bigcap_nN_n.\n\\]\nThe identity \\(E_N=E_NE_{N_n}\\) preserves every requirement with \\(N\\) in place of \\(N_{n+1}\\). Thus\n\\[\nE_N(x_j)=\\tau(x_j)1\\qquad(j\\ge1).\n\\]\nDensity gives \\(E_N(x)=\\tau(x)1\\) for all \\(x\\in M\\), so \\(N=\\mathbb C1\\).</p><p>In particular \\(R\\) is a factor. It is finite and contains full matrix algebras of unbounded size, so it is of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\), with the required increasing-matrix presentation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a unital full matrix algebra \\(F\\subset N\\), of size at least two, such that \\( \\|E_{F'\\cap N}(y)-\\tau(y)1\\|_2<\\eta. \\)", "statement": "<p>**The approximation step in the proof of original Lemma 6.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(N\\) is an arbitrary \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor with normalized trace \\(\\tau\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(y=y^*\\in N\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\eta&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Conditional expectations are trace-preserving.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a unital full matrix algebra \\(F\\subset N\\), of size at least two, such that\n\\[\n\\|E_{F'\\cap N}(y)-\\tau(y)1\\|_2&lt;\\eta.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Approximate \\(y\\) in \\(L^2\\) by a spectral step\n\\[\ny_0=\\sum_{i=1}^k\\lambda_ip_i,\n\\]\nwhere the \\(p_i\\) partition the identity.</p><p>For a large integer \\(b\\), split off \\(\\lfloor b\\tau(p_i)\\rfloor\\) subprojections of trace \\(1/b\\) in each \\(p_i\\). The remaining projection has trace\n\\[\n\\frac{b-\\sum_{i=1}^k\\lfloor b\\tau(p_i)\\rfloor}{b}\n&lt;\\frac{k}{b}.\n\\]\nThe numerator is an integer, so the remainder also splits into projections of trace \\(1/b\\). These pieces form a partition of the identity by \\(b\\) equivalent projections, hence extend to matrix units for a unital\n\\[\nF\\cong M_b(\\mathbb C).\n\\]</p><p>Define \\(y_1\\in F\\) to equal \\(\\lambda_i\\) on the pieces taken from \\(p_i\\), and zero on the remaining pieces. Then\n\\[\n\\|y-y_1\\|_2\n\\le\n\\|y-y_0\\|_2+\\|y_0\\|\\sqrt{k/b}.\n\\]\nThe expectation of \\(y_1\\) onto \\(F'\\cap N\\) is \\(\\tau(y_1)1\\). Contractivity on \\(L^2\\), together with\n\\[\n|\\tau(y-y_1)|\\le\\|y-y_1\\|_2,\n\\]\ngives the required estimate by first choosing the spectral approximation and then \\(b\\), with \\(b\\ge2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a subfactor \\(D\\subset Q^\\omega\\), generated by increasing unital full matrix algebras of unbounded sizes, which is free from the constant copy of \\(P\\) in \\(P^\\omega\\).", "statement": "<p>**Free position in the full ultrapower — original Lemma 6.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(Q\\subset P\\) is a unital irreducible inclusion of \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factors:\n\\[\nQ'\\cap P=\\mathbb C1.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\) has separable predual.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\omega\\) is a nonprincipal ultrafilter on \\(\\mathbb N\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a finite tracial von Neumann algebra \\(T\\), define\n\\[\nT^\\omega=\\ell^\\infty(\\mathbb N,T)/I_\\omega,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nI_\\omega=\n\\{(a_i):\\sup_i\\|a_i\\|&lt;\\infty,\\ \\lim_\\omega\\|a_i\\|_2=0\\}.\n\\]\nIts trace is\n\\[\n\\tau_\\omega([a_i])=\\lim_\\omega\\tau(a_i).\n\\]\nThe algebra \\(T\\) embeds by constant sequences, and \\(Q^\\omega\\subset P^\\omega\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a subfactor \\(D\\subset Q^\\omega\\), generated by increasing unital full matrix algebras of unbounded sizes, which is free from the constant copy of \\(P\\) in \\(P^\\omega\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the ultraproduct-of-subalgebras case of Popa’s independence theorem [25, Theorem 0.1(a); see also Theorem 4.3].</p><p>For von Neumann subalgebras \\(A,B\\subset M\\), write \\(A\\prec_M B\\) for Popa’s intertwining of a nonzero corner of \\(A\\) into a finite matrix amplification of \\(B\\). The theorem assumes finite factors \\(M_i\\) with \\(\\dim M_i\\to\\infty\\), and unital subalgebras \\(Q_i\\subset M_i\\) satisfying\n\\[\nQ_i\\nprec_{M_i}(Q_i'\\cap M_i).\n\\]\nWriting\n\\[\n\\mathcal M=\\prod_\\omega M_i,\\qquad\n\\mathcal Q=\\prod_\\omega Q_i,\\qquad\n\\mathcal C=\\mathcal Q'\\cap\\mathcal M,\n\\]\nit supplies, for an \\(L^2\\)-separable subspace\n\\[\nX\\subset\\mathcal M\\ominus\\mathcal C,\n\\]\na diffuse abelian algebra \\(A\\subset\\mathcal Q\\) such that\n\\[\nE_{\\mathcal C}(x_0a_1x_1\\cdots a_nx_n)=0\n\\]\nwhenever \\(n\\ge1\\), \\(x_0\\in X\\cup\\{1\\}\\), \\(x_i\\in X\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le n\\), and \\(a_i\\in A\\ominus\\mathbb C1\\).</p><p>In particular, when \\(\\mathcal C=\\mathbb C1\\) and \\(T\\subset\\mathcal M\\) has separable predual, apply the theorem with \\(X=T\\ominus\\mathbb C1\\). The moment identities, traciality, and decomposition into scalar and centered parts show that \\(A\\) is free from \\(T\\). Separability here is for \\(L^2\\), as in [25, Section 1.1].</p><p>Check the hypotheses for the constant sequence \\(M_i=P\\), \\(Q_i=Q\\). The dimension condition holds because these factors are infinite-dimensional. If\n\\[\nQ\\prec_P\\mathbb C1,\n\\]\nthe intertwining data would give a nonzero normal \\(*\\)-homomorphism from a nonzero corner of \\(Q\\) into a finite-dimensional matrix algebra. A nonzero homomorphism from a factor is faithful, whereas a \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor has arbitrarily large families of nonzero orthogonal projections. This is impossible. Since \\(Q'\\cap P=\\mathbb C1\\), the non-intertwining condition follows.</p><p>Next verify\n\\[\n(Q^\\omega)'\\cap P^\\omega=\\mathbb C1.\n\\]\nLet \\((x_i)\\) be a uniformly bounded representative of an element of this relative commutant. Commutation with all sequences of unitaries from \\(Q\\) implies\n\\[\nd_i:=\\sup_{v\\in\\mathcal U(Q)}\\|vx_iv^*-x_i\\|_2\n\\longrightarrow_\\omega0.\n\\]\nOtherwise choose a witnessing unitary at each coordinate on an \\(\\omega\\)-large set.</p><p>The \\(L^2\\)-closed convex hull of the \\(Q\\)-unitary orbit of \\(x_i\\) has a unique minimum-norm vector. It is \\(Q\\)-central, has trace \\(\\tau(x_i)\\), and belongs to \\(P\\): the hull remains in the operator-norm ball of radius \\(\\|x_i\\|\\), which is \\(L^2\\)-closed by weak-* compactness and trace pairings. Irreducibility makes the vector \\(\\tau(x_i)1\\). Every point in the hull is within \\(d_i\\) of \\(x_i\\), so\n\\[\n\\|x_i-\\tau(x_i)1\\|_2\\le d_i.\n\\]\nBounded scalar sequences equal their scalar ultralimit in the tracial ultrapower. This proves the full relative-commutant assertion, concerning all of \\(Q^\\omega\\), not only constant \\(Q\\).</p><p>Apply Popa’s theorem to constant \\(P\\), and choose a Haar unitary \\(u_1\\) in the resulting diffuse abelian algebra. Then \\(u_1\\in Q^\\omega\\) is free from \\(P\\).</p><p>The algebra \\(W^*(P,u_1)\\) has separable \\(L^2\\)-space: countably many words in generators of \\(P\\), \\(u_1\\), and their adjoints have dense linear span. A second application gives a Haar unitary \\(u_2\\in Q^\\omega\\) free from \\(W^*(P,u_1)\\).</p><p>Expansion of centered reduced words shows that\n\\[\nP,\\quad W^*(u_1),\\quad W^*(u_2)\n\\]\nare jointly free. Thus \\(W^*(u_1,u_2)\\) is free from \\(P\\) and is trace-isomorphic to \\(L(\\mathbb F_2)\\). This is a separable-predual \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor: all nontrivial conjugacy classes of \\(\\mathbb F_2\\) are infinite, so a central square-summable Fourier coefficient function is supported at the identity.</p><p>Apply Proposition 4 inside \\(W^*(u_1,u_2)\\) to obtain \\(D\\). Freeness passes to \\(D\\), completing the proof in the ambient tracial ultrapower \\(P^\\omega\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\|[Y^{(h)},X]\\| \\le(3c_1+2)g_M(Y)\\|X\\|. \\) The constant is independent of \\(M,r,h\\).", "statement": "<p>**Finite standard multiplicities — original Proposition 6.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(M\\) is a separable-predual \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(r\\ge1\\) is finite, and \\(M\\) acts on\n\\[\nH_r=L^2(M)^r\n\\]\nby common left multiplication.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(Y\\in B(H_r)\\), and\n\\[\ng_M(Y)=\\sup_{\\|a\\|\\le1,\\ a\\in M}\\|[Y,L_a]\\|.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(h\\ge1\\) is finite, and \\(X\\in M_h(M)\\) acts entrywise on \\(H_r^h\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(c_1\\) is the constant in Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Traces on matrix amplifications are normalized.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\|[Y^{(h)},X]\\|\n\\le(3c_1+2)g_M(Y)\\|X\\|.\n\\]\nThe constant is independent of \\(M,r,h\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(\\delta=g_M(Y)\\). By homogeneity, assume \\(\\|X\\|\\le1\\).</p><p>Choose \\(h_0\\ge h\\) such that \\(m=2h_0\\ge r\\), extend \\(Y\\) by zero to\n\\[\nH_m=L^2(M)^m,\n\\]\nand retain the notation \\(Y\\). The copy projection reduces the common action of \\(M\\), so the scalar commutator bound remains \\(\\delta\\).</p><p>Set\n\\[\nX'=X\\oplus0\\in M_{h_0}(M),\\qquad P=M_m(M),\n\\]\nand define\n\\[\ns=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n(1-X'X'^*)^{1/2}&amp;X'\\\\\nX'^*&amp;-(1-X'^*X')^{1/2}\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nFunctional calculus gives\n\\[\n(1-X'X'^*)^{1/2}X'\n=\nX'(1-X'^*X')^{1/2},\n\\]\nso \\(s=s^*=s^{-1}\\).</p><p>Traciality gives equal moments for \\(X'X'^*\\) and \\(X'^*X'\\), including the zeroth moment. Polynomial approximation on \\([0,1]\\) gives equal traces for the square roots above. Thus\n\\[\n\\tau_P(s)=0.\n\\]</p><p>Write vectors of \\(H_m^m\\) as arrays \\((\\xi_{ij})\\), with \\(i\\) the outer amplification coordinate and \\(j\\) the copy coordinate. The map\n\\[\n(\\xi_{ij})\\longmapsto\\sqrt m\\,(\\xi_{ij})\n\\]\nis a unitary \\(H_m^m\\to L^2(P)\\), because on \\(L^2(P)\\)\n\\[\n\\|(\\xi_{ij})\\|_2^2\n=m^{-1}\\sum_{i,j}\\|\\xi_{ij}\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nThe matrix action of \\(P\\) becomes left multiplication. The upper-right block of \\([Y^{(m)},L_s]\\) is the commutator with \\(X'\\). Restricting row coordinates to the original \\(h\\) and copy coordinates to the original \\(r\\) recovers the original \\([Y^{(h)},X]\\). It is enough to bound \\([Y^{(m)},L_s]\\).</p><p>Choose \\(R\\subset M\\) from Proposition 4, with matrix stages \\(F_n\\). Average \\(Y\\) on \\(H_m\\) over \\(\\mathcal U(F_n)\\), using common left multiplication, and take a weak operator cluster point \\(Y_0\\). For each averaged unitary \\(v\\),\n\\[\n\\|L_vYL_v^*-Y\\|\\le\\delta.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n[Y_0,L_R]=0,\\qquad\n\\|Y_0-Y\\|\\le\\delta,\\qquad\ng_M(Y_0)\\le3\\delta.\n\\]\nThe commutation extends from matrix stages to \\(R\\) by strong density; the bounds pass through weak operator limits.</p><p>On \\(L^2(P)=H_m^m\\), put\n\\[\nS=Y_0^{(m)},\\qquad Q=M_m(R).\n\\]\nMatrix units and \\(R'\\cap M=\\mathbb C1\\) give \\(Q'\\cap P=\\mathbb C1\\). Moreover \\(S\\) commutes with \\(L_Q\\).</p><p>For \\(e_0=e_{11}\\otimes1\\), the space \\(e_0L^2(P)\\) is one row of \\(m\\) standard copies, with \\(e_0Pe_0\\cong M\\) acting by common left multiplication. Since \\(S\\) commutes with \\(L_{e_0}\\),\n\\[\n\\|[S,L_z]\\|\\le3\\delta\\|z\\|\n\\qquad(z\\in e_0Pe_0).\n\\]\nIf \\(f\\in Q\\) is a projection with \\(\\tau_P(f)\\le1/m\\), comparison in the factor \\(Q\\) gives a unitary \\(v\\in Q\\) with\n\\[\nf\\le ve_0v^*.\n\\]\nConjugating by \\(L_v\\), which commutes with \\(S\\), gives\n\\[\n\\|[S,L_z]\\|\\le3\\delta\\|z\\|\n\\qquad\n(z\\in fPf,\\ f\\in Q,\\ \\tau_P(f)\\le1/m).\n\\]</p><p>Fix a nonprincipal ultrafilter \\(\\omega\\). Distinguish the tracial ultrapower\n\\[\nP^\\omega=\\ell^\\infty(\\mathbb N,P)/I_\\omega\n\\]\nfrom the Hilbert ultrapower\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=(L^2(P))_\\omega^{\\mathrm{Hilb}}.\n\\]\nSending the class of a uniformly operator-bounded sequence \\((a_i)\\) to its Hilbert-ultrapower class is a trace-preserving isometry on the dense subspace \\(P^\\omega\\subset L^2(P^\\omega)\\). Its extension identifies\n\\[\n\\mathcal C=L^2(P^\\omega)\n\\]\nwith a closed subspace of \\(\\mathcal H\\).</p><p>For a bounded sequence \\((a_i)\\) in \\(P\\), its coordinatewise left action and the adjoint action corresponding to \\((a_i^*)\\) preserve the bounded-algebra-sequence subspace of \\(\\mathcal C\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal C\\) reduces these actions. If \\((a_i)\\in I_\\omega\\), then for every operator-bounded \\((b_i)\\),\n\\[\n\\|a_ib_i\\|_2\\le\\|b_i\\|\\|a_i\\|_2\\longrightarrow_\\omega0.\n\\]\nThus the action on \\(\\mathcal C\\) factors through \\(P^\\omega\\) and is left multiplication by \\([a_i]\\). This factoring is needed only on \\(\\mathcal C\\), not on all of \\(\\mathcal H\\).</p><p>Let \\(S_\\omega^{\\mathrm{Hilb}}\\) be the coordinatewise action of \\(S\\) on \\(\\mathcal H\\), and put\n\\[\n\\widehat S=P_{\\mathcal C}S_\\omega^{\\mathrm{Hilb}}|_{\\mathcal C}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(S\\) commutes with all left multipliers from \\(Q\\), its Hilbert ultrapower commutes with every bounded left \\(Q\\)-sequence. Compression gives\n\\[\n[\\widehat S,L_{Q^\\omega}]=0.\n\\]</p><p>Set \\(t=1/(2m)\\). We claim\n\\[\n\\|[\\widehat S,L_z]\\|\\le3\\delta\\|z\\|\n\\tag{37}\n\\]\nwhenever \\(e\\in Q^\\omega\\) is a projection with \\(\\tau(e)=t\\) and \\(z\\in eP^\\omega e\\).</p><p>To lift \\(e\\), choose a bounded self-adjoint representative \\((a_i)\\) in \\(Q\\), and set\n\\[\nf_i=1_{[1/2,\\infty)}(a_i).\n\\]\nThe scalar inequality\n\\[\n|x-1_{[1/2,\\infty)}(x)|\\le2|x^2-x|\n\\]\ngives \\(\\|f_i-a_i\\|_2\\to_\\omega0\\). Thus \\([f_i]=e\\) and\n\\[\n\\tau(f_i)\\longrightarrow_\\omega t.\n\\]\nSince \\(t&lt;1/m\\), replace \\(f_i\\) by zero off an \\(\\omega\\)-large set to arrange \\(\\tau(f_i)\\le1/m\\) for every \\(i\\).</p><p>Given \\(z\\in eP^\\omega e\\) and \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), the \\(C^*\\)-quotient norm gives a representative \\((b_i)\\) with\n\\[\n\\sup_i\\|b_i\\|\\le\\|z\\|+\\eta.\n\\]\nThen \\(z_i=f_ib_if_i\\) represents \\(z\\), lies in the required corner, and has the same norm bound. Therefore\n\\[\n\\|[S,L_{z_i}]\\|\\le3\\delta(\\|z\\|+\\eta)\n\\]\nfor every \\(i\\). Pass to the Hilbert ultrapower and compress to the reducing subspace \\(\\mathcal C\\). The compression is \\([\\widehat S,L_z]\\). Letting \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\) proves (37).</p><p>By Proposition 5, choose \\(D\\subset Q^\\omega\\) free from constant \\(P\\), and set\n\\[\nL=W^*(D,P)\\subset P^\\omega.\n\\]\nFaithfulness of the trace and reduced-word inner products identify \\(L\\) with the tracial free product \\(D*P\\).</p><p>Let\n\\[\nG=P_{L^2(L)}\\widehat S|_{L^2(L)}.\n\\]\nThe subspace \\(L^2(L)\\) reduces left multiplication by \\(L\\). Consequently \\(G\\) commutes with \\(L_D\\), and (37) gives the hypotheses of Proposition 3 with\n\\[\nA_0=P,\\qquad \\epsilon=3\\delta,\\qquad t=1/(2m).\n\\]</p><p>Every constant \\(L^2(P)\\)-vector belongs to \\(\\mathcal C\\), by approximation by bounded vectors from \\(P\\), and \\(S_\\omega^{\\mathrm{Hilb}}\\) sends constant \\(\\xi\\) to constant \\(S\\xi\\). Both vectors also belong to \\(L^2(L)\\), so both compressions preserve the action of \\(S\\) on constants. Since \\(L_s\\) preserves the constant subspace,\n\\[\n\\langle[G,L_s]\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\n=\n\\langle[S,L_s]\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\n\\qquad(\\xi,\\eta\\in L^2(P)).\n\\]\nApply Proposition 3 to the trace-zero symmetry \\(s\\in P\\):\n\\[\n\\|[S,L_s]\\|\\le3c_1\\delta.\n\\]</p><p>Replacing \\(Y_0\\) by \\(Y\\) costs at most\n\\[\n2\\|Y_0-Y\\|\\|s\\|\\le2\\delta,\n\\]\nsince Hilbert amplification preserves the difference norm. The initial block compression now yields\n\\[\n\\|[Y^{(h)},X]\\|\\le(3c_1+2)\\delta.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\|[Y^{(h)},X]\\| \\le C_{\\mathrm{fac}}g_P(Y)\\|X\\|. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Representations of factors — original Proposition 7.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\subset B(K)\\) is a unital factor acting on a separable complex Hilbert space.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(Y\\in B(K)\\), \\(h\\ge1\\), and \\(X\\in M_h(P)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(g_P(Y)\\) as in Theorem 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n\\[\nC_{\\mathrm{fac}}\n=\n\\max\\{3c_1+2,\\ 1+2c_{\\mathrm{row}},\\ 2\\},\n\\tag{38}\n\\]\nwhere \\(c_1\\) and \\(c_{\\mathrm{row}}\\) are from Propositions 3 and 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\|[Y^{(h)},X]\\|\n\\le C_{\\mathrm{fac}}g_P(Y)\\|X\\|.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First treat a \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor \\(M\\) in an arbitrary normal representation on a separable Hilbert space. Its predual is separable.</p><p>Every cyclic normal representation embeds into the tracial standard representation. Indeed, if \\(\\xi\\) is cyclic, its normal positive functional has a trace density \\(a\\in L^1(M)_+\\) [14, Corollary 5.14 and Remark 5.15]. The map\n\\[\nx\\xi\\longmapsto xa^{1/2}\n\\]\nis isometric, since both squared norms equal the functional evaluated at \\(x^*x\\). It extends to an intertwiner with reducing range.</p><p>Decompose the given representation into orthogonal cyclic reducing subspaces. There are at most countably many, so it embeds into\n\\[\n\\widetilde K=L^2(M)^{\\oplus\\mathbb N}.\n\\]\nExtend \\(Y\\) by zero off the embedded reducing subspace; the scalar commutator bound is unchanged.</p><p>Let \\(p_n\\) project onto the first \\(n\\) standard copies. It commutes with the common left action, and\n\\[\n[p_nYp_n,L_x]=p_n[Y,L_x]p_n.\n\\]\nProposition 6 applies on \\(p_n\\widetilde K\\), with constant \\(3c_1+2\\). For each fixed finite matrix test, the compressed commutators converge strongly to the full commutator. The uniform norm bound passes to the strong limit. Restricting to the original reducing subspace proves the \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) case.</p><p>If \\(P\\) is properly infinite, choose isometries \\(v_1,\\ldots,v_h\\in P\\) with orthogonal ranges, and put\n\\[\nV=[v_1\\ \\cdots\\ v_h].\n\\]\nThen \\(V^*V=I_{K^h}\\). Put \\(\\delta=g_P(Y)\\). Apply the row and column derivation estimates of Proposition 1 to \\(a\\mapsto[Y,a]\\):\n\\[\n\\|YV-VY^{(h)}\\|\\le c_{\\mathrm{row}}\\delta,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|Y^{(h)}V^*-V^*Y\\|\\le c_{\\mathrm{row}}\\delta.\n\\]\nFor \\(X\\in M_h(P)\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n[Y,VXV^*]\n&amp;=(YV-VY^{(h)})XV^*\n+V[Y^{(h)},X]V^*\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad+VX(Y^{(h)}V^*-V^*Y).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSince \\(\\|VXV^*\\|=\\|X\\|\\), this gives\n\\[\n\\|[Y^{(h)},X]\\|\n\\le(1+2c_{\\mathrm{row}})\\delta\\|X\\|.\n\\]\nConjugation by the isometry \\(V:K^h\\to K\\) preserves the norm of the middle term whether or not its range is all of \\(K\\).</p><p>Finally, if \\(P\\) is a finite type I factor, average \\(Y\\) over its compact unitary group to obtain \\(Y_0\\in P'\\) with\n\\[\n\\|Y-Y_0\\|\\le\\delta.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n[Y^{(h)},X]=[(Y-Y_0)^{(h)},X],\n\\]\ngiving the constant \\(2\\).</p><p>These cases exhaust the factors.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\|[Y^{(h)},X]\\| \\le Cg_P(Y)\\|X\\|. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Central decomposition on separable Hilbert spaces — original Proposition 7.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\subset B(K)\\) is a unital von Neumann algebra on a separable complex Hilbert space.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(Y\\in B(K)\\), \\(h\\ge1\\), and \\(X\\in M_h(P)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(g_P(Y)\\) as in Theorem 2.</p><p class=\"li\">With \\(C_{\\mathrm{fac}}\\) from Proposition 7, set\n\\[\nC=3C_{\\mathrm{fac}}+2.\n\\tag{38}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\|[Y^{(h)},X]\\|\n\\le Cg_P(Y)\\|X\\|.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(\\delta=g_P(Y)\\). Average \\(Y\\) over the compact unitary groups of the finite-dimensional algebras generated by finite families of central projections. Direct the families by inclusion and take a cofinal weak operator cluster limit \\(Y_1\\).</p><p>The resulting operator commutes with every central projection, hence with \\(Z(P)\\), and\n\\[\n\\|Y_1-Y\\|\\le\\delta,\\qquad\ng_P(Y_1)\\le3\\delta.\n\\tag{39}\n\\]\nFor the first bound, every averaged unitary lies in \\(P\\), so its conjugation changes \\(Y\\) by at most \\(\\delta\\). Averages and weak operator limits preserve the bound. The second follows from\n\\[\ng_P(Y_1)\\le g_P(Y)+2\\|Y_1-Y\\|.\n\\]</p><p>Use central direct-integral decomposition on the separable Hilbert space:\n\\[\nK=\\int^\\oplus K_\\alpha\\,d\\mu(\\alpha),\\qquad\nP=\\int^\\oplus P_\\alpha\\,d\\mu(\\alpha),\n\\]\nwith factor fibers \\(P_\\alpha\\) almost everywhere and separable \\(K_\\alpha\\) [28; 1, Sections III.1.6.1–4 and III.5.1.10–15].</p><p>The center is the scalar diagonal algebra, whose commutant consists of decomposable operators. Thus \\(Y_1\\) has fibers \\(Y_1(\\alpha)\\). We verify\n\\[\ng_{P_\\alpha}(Y_1(\\alpha))\\le3\\delta\n\\quad\\text{for almost every }\\alpha.\n\\tag{40}\n\\]</p><p>Fix countably many bounded measurable sections whose values generate \\(P_\\alpha\\) almost everywhere. Take all rational complex \\(*\\)-polynomials in these sections, including rational scalar constants. For each resulting section \\(b\\in P\\), form\n\\[\nc_b=b(1+b^*b)^{-1/2}\\in P.\n\\]\nOn each fiber where the generators have the stated property, these contractions are norm dense in the contraction ball of the generated \\(C^*\\)-algebra. Indeed, the polynomials are norm dense, the transformation is norm continuous, and its range contains the open unit ball, with inverse\n\\[\nc\\longmapsto c(1-c^*c)^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nThe closed unit ball is the norm closure of the open one. Kaplansky density therefore makes these countably many fiber contractions strongly dense in the unit ball of \\(P_\\alpha\\).</p><p>For each \\(c_b\\), the estimate\n\\[\n\\|[Y_1,c_b]\\|\\le3\\delta\n\\]\ngives the same fiber estimate outside a null set, by the essential-supremum norm formula. Remove the countable union of these null sets. Strong approximation by the contraction tests passes the estimate to every element of the fiber unit ball, proving (40).</p><p>Apply Proposition 7 on this conull set. For fixed \\(X\\in M_h(P)\\), its entries are decomposable, and the essential-supremum norm formula gives\n\\[\n\\|[Y_1^{(h)},X]\\|\n\\le3C_{\\mathrm{fac}}\\delta\\|X\\|.\n\\]\nRestoring \\(Y\\) costs at most \\(2\\delta\\|X\\|\\), by (39). Hence the constant is \\(C=3C_{\\mathrm{fac}}+2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a separable common reducing subspace \\(K_0\\subset K\\) for \\(A_X\\) and \\(Y\\), containing every \\(\\xi_i\\), such that, on writing \\( \\sigma:A_X\\to B(K_0),\\qquad Y_0=Y|_{K_0},\\qquad P_0=\\sigma(A_X)'', \\) one has …", "statement": "<p>**Separable localization of a finite matrix test — the localization step in the original proof of Theorem 1.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\subset B(K)\\) is an arbitrary unital von Neumann algebra; no separability is assumed.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(Y\\in B(K)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(h\\ge1\\), \\(X\\in M_h(P)\\), and\n\\[\n\\xi=(\\xi_1,\\ldots,\\xi_h)\\in K^h\n\\]\nis a unit vector.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(A_X\\subset P\\) is the unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra generated by the entries of \\(X\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a separable common reducing subspace \\(K_0\\subset K\\) for \\(A_X\\) and \\(Y\\), containing every \\(\\xi_i\\), such that, on writing\n\\[\n\\sigma:A_X\\to B(K_0),\\qquad\nY_0=Y|_{K_0},\\qquad\nP_0=\\sigma(A_X)'',\n\\]\none has\n\\[\ng_{P_0}(Y_0)\\le g_P(Y),\\qquad\n\\|\\sigma_h(X)\\|\\le\\|X\\|,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n[Y_0^{(h)},\\sigma_h(X)]\\xi\n=\n[Y^{(h)},X]\\xi,\n\\]\nwhere \\(K_0^h\\) is identified with its subspace of \\(K^h\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The \\(C^*\\)-algebra in \\(B(K)\\) generated by the entries of \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) is norm separable. The closed span of its orbits of the finitely many \\(\\xi_i\\) is a separable common reducing subspace \\(K_0\\) for \\(A_X\\) and \\(Y\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\sigma,Y_0,P_0\\) be as stated. For \\(a\\in A_X\\),\n\\[\n\\|[Y_0,\\sigma(a)]\\|\n\\le g_P(Y)\\|a\\|.\n\\]\nThe \\(C^*\\)-quotient norm of the image gives, for \\(b\\in\\sigma(A_X)\\),\n\\[\n\\|[Y_0,b]\\|\n\\le\ng_P(Y)\\inf_{\\sigma(a)=b}\\|a\\|\n=\ng_P(Y)\\|b\\|.\n\\]\nKaplansky density extends this bound to \\(P_0\\), so\n\\[\ng_{P_0}(Y_0)\\le g_P(Y).\n\\]\nThe \\(*\\)-representation \\(\\sigma\\) is completely contractive, giving\n\\(\\|\\sigma_h(X)\\|\\le\\|X\\|\\).</p><p>Finally, \\(K_0\\) reduces all operators in the test and contains every component of \\(\\xi\\). Thus the restricted commutator on \\(\\xi\\) is the original commutator.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\|\\Delta\\|_{\\mathrm{cb}}\\le C\\|\\Delta\\|. \\) Moreover, there is \\(V\\in B(K)\\) such that \\( \\Delta(a)=V\\sigma(a)-\\sigma(a)V \\qquad(a\\in A). \\)", "statement": "<p>**Derivations and similarity — original Proposition 7.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(A\\) is a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\sigma:A\\to B(K)\\) is a unital \\(*\\)-representation on an arbitrary complex Hilbert space.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\Delta:A\\to B(K)\\) is a bounded \\(\\sigma\\)-derivation:\n\\[\n\\Delta(ab)=\\Delta(a)\\sigma(b)+\\sigma(a)\\Delta(b).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(C=3C_{\\mathrm{fac}}+2\\) is the numerical constant defined in Proposition 8.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\|\\Delta\\|_{\\mathrm{cb}}\\le C\\|\\Delta\\|.\n\\]\nMoreover, there is \\(V\\in B(K)\\) such that\n\\[\n\\Delta(a)=V\\sigma(a)-\\sigma(a)V\n\\qquad(a\\in A).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Decompose \\(K\\) into cyclic reducing subspaces for \\(\\sigma\\):\n\\[\nK=\\bigoplus_{i\\in I}K_i.\n\\]\nA maximal orthogonal family gives such a decomposition: a nonzero vector in its orthogonal complement would generate another cyclic reducing subspace. The index set \\(I\\) may have any cardinality. Let \\(q_i\\) project onto \\(K_i\\).</p><p>For each \\(i\\), the map\n\\[\na\\longmapsto\\Delta(a)|_{K_i}\n\\]\nis a rectangular derivation with cyclic domain representation \\(\\sigma|_{K_i}\\) and full output representation \\(\\sigma\\). Proposition 1 gives a bounded map \\(V_i:K_i\\to K\\) such that\n\\[\n\\Delta(a)|_{K_i}\n=\nV_i\\sigma(a)|_{K_i}-\\sigma(a)V_i.\n\\]</p><p>For a finite subset \\(F\\subset I\\), put\n\\[\nq_F=\\sum_{i\\in F}q_i,\\qquad\nV_F=\\sum_{i\\in F}V_iq_i\\in B(K).\n\\]\nEach \\(V_F\\) is bounded, and the reducing property gives\n\\[\n[V_F,\\sigma(a)]=\\Delta(a)q_F\n\\qquad(a\\in A).\n\\tag{41}\n\\]\nOnly the scalar commutator norm, not \\(\\|V_F\\|\\), needs to be uniform in \\(F\\).</p><p>For \\(b\\in\\sigma(A)\\), take the infimum over lifts in (41):\n\\[\n\\|[V_F,b]\\|\\le\\|\\Delta\\|\\|b\\|.\n\\]\nThis uses the \\(C^*\\)-quotient norm of the \\(*\\)-image and does not require faithfulness of \\(\\sigma\\). Kaplansky density gives\n\\[\ng_{\\sigma(A)''}(V_F)\\le\\|\\Delta\\|.\n\\]</p><p>Propositions 8 and 9 together apply the separable commutator estimate to each finite matrix test on the arbitrary Hilbert space. Consequently, for \\(X\\in M_h(A)\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\Delta_h(X)q_F^{(h)}\\|\n&amp;=\\|[V_F^{(h)},\\sigma_h(X)]\\|\\\\\n&amp;\\le C\\|\\Delta\\|\\|\\sigma_h(X)\\|\\\\\n&amp;\\le C\\|\\Delta\\|\\|X\\|.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe projections \\(q_F\\), directed by finite subsets of \\(I\\), converge strongly to the identity. For each fixed \\(h,X\\), the bound therefore passes to \\(\\Delta_h(X)\\). Taking the supremum over \\(h,X\\) proves complete boundedness.</p><p>The completely bounded implementation assertion of Proposition 1, applied with the same domain and output representation \\(\\sigma\\), supplies the bounded implementing operator.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 2, "cite"], [3, 7, "cite"], [3, 8, "cite"], [3, 13, "cite"], [3, 22, "cite"], [3, 25, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [6, 3, "cite"], [7, 0, "cite"], [8, 21, "cite"], [8, 22, "cite"], [9, 13, "cite"], [10, 8, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [10, 12, "cite"], [10, 13, "cite"], [10, 14, "cite"], [11, 10, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [12, 8, "cite"], [12, 14, "cite"], [12, 15, "cite"], [13, 12, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [15, 8, "cite"], [16, 8, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [17, 8, "cite"], [18, 20, "cite"], [18, 21, "cite"], [19, 13, "cite"], [19, 18, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [23, 1, "cite"], [23, 25, "cite"], [24, 1, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [25, 0, "cite"]]});
